SDAT†book.namevalueHex Notebookcomment·The Hexbook ‚Äî every text below is a page of it, ported from Hex Casting (MIT) with Patchouli's markup as Yog'scategory.basicsGetting Started)category.basics.descÎThe practitioners of this art would cast their so-called {#b38ef3|Hexes} by drawing strange patterns in the air with a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|Staff}} -- or craft {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|powerful magical items}} to do the casting for them. How might I do the same?!category.castingHex Casting+category.casting.desc£I've started to understand how the old masters cast their {#b38ef3|Hexes}! It's a bit complicated, but I'm sure I can figure it out. Let's see...%category.greatworkThe Great Work/category.greatwork.descÖI have seen... so much. I have... experienced... annihilation and deconstruction and reconstruction. I have seen the atoms of the world screaming as they were inverted and subverted and demoted to energy. I have seen I have seen I have sget stick bugged lmao!category.interop/Cross-Mod Compatibility+category.interop.descªIt appears I have installed some mods Hexcasting interoperates with! I've detailed them here.category.itemsItems'category.items.descµI devote this section to the magical and mysterious items I might encounter in my studies.category.lore	Lore%category.lore.desc”I have uncovered some letters and text not of direct relevance to my art. But, I think I may be able to divine some of the history of the world from these. Let me see...#category.patternsPatterns-category.patterns.descâA list of all the patterns I've discovered, as well as what they do.+category.great_spellsGreat Spells5category.great_spells.descçThe spells catalogued here are purported to be of legendary difficulty and power. They seem to have been recorded only {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/scroll|sparsely} (for good reason, the texts claim). It's probably just the ramblings of extinct traditionalists, though -- a pattern's a pattern.

What could possibly go wrong?category.spellsSpells)category.spells.descÅPatterns and actions that perform a magical effect on the world.)page.couldnt_cast.n1ïArgh! Why won't it let me cast the spell?!

The scroll I found rings with authenticity. I can {i|feel} it humming in the scroll-- the pattern is true, or as true as it can be. The spell is {i|right there}.

But it feels as if it's on the other side of some thin membrane. I called it-- it tried to manifest-- yet it {i|COULD NOT}.)page.couldnt_cast.n2ãIt felt like the barrier may have weakened ever so slightly from the force that I exerted on the spell; yet despite my greatest efforts-- my deepest focus, my finest amethyst, my precisest drawings-- it {i|refuses} to cross the barrier. It's maddening.

{i|This} is where my arcane studies end? Cursed by impotence, cursed to lose my rightful powers?

I should take a deep breath. I should meditate on what I have learned, even if it wasn't very much...)page.couldnt_cast.n3ü	...After careful reflection... I have discovered a change in myself.

It seems... in lieu of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|amethyst}}, I've unlocked the ability to cast spells using my own mind and life energy-- just as I read of in the legends of old.

I'm not sure why I can now. It's just... the truth-knowledge-burden was always there, and I see it now. I know it. I bear it.

Fortunately, I feel my limits as well-- I would get approximately two {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst}}'s worth of {#74b3f2|media} out of my health at its prime.)page.couldnt_cast.n4ÉI shudder to even consider it-- I've kept my mind mostly intact so far, in my studies. But the fact is-- I form one side of a tenuous link.

I'm connected to some other side-- a side whose boundary has thinned from that trauma. A place where simple actions spell out eternal glory.

Is it so wrong, to want it for myself?%entry.couldnt_castA Frustrationpage.geodes.n1≈Aha! While mining deep underground, I found an enormous geode resonating with energy-- energy which pressed against my skull and my thoughts. And now, I hold that pressure in my hand, in solid form. That proves it. This {i|must} be the place spoken about in legends where {#74b3f2|media} accumulates.

These {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|amethyst crystals}} must be a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{#8d6acc|convenient, solidified form of {#74b3f2|Media}}}.page.geodes.n2ÕIt appears that, in addition to the {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Shards}} I have seen in the past, these crystals can also drop bits of powdered {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}, as well as these {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst Crystals}}. It looks like I'll have a better chance of finding the {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst Crystals}} by using a Fortune pickaxe.page.geodes.n3±As I take the beauty of the crystal in, I can feel connections flashing wildly in my mind. It's like the {#74b3f2|media} in the air is entering me, empowering me, elucidating me... It feels wonderful.

Finally, my study into the arcane is starting to make some sense!

Let me reread those old legends again, now that I know what I'm looking at.entry.geodesGeodespage.media.n1ë{#74b3f2|Media} is a form of mental energy external to a mind. All living creatures generate trace amounts of {#74b3f2|media} when thinking about anything; after the thought is finished, the media is released into the environment.

The art of casting {#b38ef3|Hexes} is all about manipulating {#74b3f2|media} to do your bidding.page.media.n2€{#74b3f2|Media} can exert influences on other media-- the strength and type of influence can be manipulated by drawing {#74b3f2|media} out into patterns.

Scholars of the art used a concentrated blob of {#74b3f2|media} on the end of a stick: by waving it in the air in precise configurations, they were able to manipulate enough {#74b3f2|media} with enough precision to influence the world itself, in the form of a {#b38ef3|Hex}.page.media.n3”Sadly, even a fully sentient being (like myself, presumably) can only generate miniscule amounts of {#74b3f2|media}. It would be quite impractical to try and use my own brainpower to cast Hexes.

But legend has it that there are underground deposits where {#74b3f2|media} slowly accumulates, growing into crystalline forms.

If I could just find one of those...entry.mediaMedia)page.start_to_see.n1[The texts weren't lying. Nature took its due.)page.start_to_see.n2âThat... that was...

...that was one of the {i|worst} things I've {i|ever} experienced. I offered my plan to Nature, and got a firm smile and a tearing sensation in return-- a piece of myself breaking away, like amethyst dust in the rain.

I feel lucky to have {i|survived}, much less have the sagacity to write this-- I should declare the matter closed, double-check my math before I cast any more {#b38ef3|Hexes}, and never make such a mistake again.)page.start_to_see.n3ô...But.

But for the scarcest instant, that part of myself... it {i|saw}... {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/the_work|{#8d6acc|something}}. A place-- a design, perhaps? (Such distinctions didn't seem to matter in the face of... that.)

And a... a membrane-barrier-skin-border, separating myself from a realm of raw thought-flow-light-energy. I remember-- I saw-thought-recalled-felt-- the barrier fuzzing at its edges, just so slightly.

I wanted {i|through.})page.start_to_see.n4µI shouldn't. I {i|know} I shouldn't. It's dangerous. It's too dangerous. The force required... I'd have to bring myself within a hair's breadth of Death itself with a {i|single stroke}.

But I'm. So. {i|Close}.

{i|This} is the culmination of my art. This is the {#54398a|Enlightenment} I've been seeking.

I want more. I need to see it again. I {i|will} see it.

What is my mortal mind against immortal glory?%entry.start_to_seeWHAT DID I SEEpage.n101.n1≥Casting a {#b38ef3|Hex} is quite difficult-- no wonder this art was lost to time! I'll have to re-read my notes carefully.

I can start a {#b38ef3|Hex} by pressing with a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|Staff}} in my hand-- this will cause a hexagonal grid of dots to appear in front of me. Then I can click and drag from dot to dot to draw patterns in the {#74b3f2|media} of the grid; finishing a pattern will run its corresponding action (more on that later).page.n101.n2°Once I've drawn enough patterns to cast a spell, the grid will disappear as the {#74b3f2|media} I've stored up is released. Holding while using my {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|staff}} will also clear the grid.

So how do patterns work? In short:
‚Ä¢ {i|Patterns} will execute...
‚Ä¢ {i|Actions}, which manipulate...
‚Ä¢ {link=@entry:hexcasting:casting/stack|{i|The Stack}}, which is a list of...
‚Ä¢ {i|Iotas}, which are simply units of information.page.n101.n3ÈFirst, {#8d6acc|patterns}. These are essential-- they're what I use to manipulate the {#74b3f2|media} around me. Certain patterns, when drawn, will cause {#8d6acc|actions} to happen. Actions are what actually {i|do} the magic; all patterns influence {#74b3f2|media} in particular ways, and when those influences end up doing something useful, we call it an action.

{#74b3f2|Media} can be fickle: if I draw an invalid pattern, I'll get some {#8d6acc|garbage} result somewhere on my stack (read on...)page.n101.n4ΩIt's interesting to note that the {i|rotation} of a pattern doesn't seem to matter at all. These two patterns both perform an action called {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Mind's Reflection}}, for example.'page.n101.n4.headerAn Examplepage.n101.n5∑A {#b38ef3|Hex} is cast by drawing (valid) actions in sequence. Each action might do one of a few things:
‚Ä¢ Gather some information about the environment, leaving it on the top of the stack;
‚Ä¢ manipulate the info gathered (e.g. adding two numbers); or
‚Ä¢ perform some magical effect, like summoning lightning or an explosion. (These actions are called "spells.")

When I start casting a {#b38ef3|Hex}, it creates an empty stack. Actions manipulate the top of that stack.page.n101.n6…For example, {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Mind's Reflection}} will create an iota representing {i|me}, the caster, and add it to the top of the stack. {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Compass' Purification}} will take the iota at the top the stack, if it represents an entity, and transform it into an iota representing that entity's location.

So, drawing those patterns in that order would result in an iota on the stack representing my position.page.n101.n7ü{#8d6acc|Iotas} can represent things like myself or my position, but there are several other types I can manipulate with {#8d6acc|Actions}. Here's a comprehensive list:
‚Ä¢ Numbers (which some legends called "doubles");
‚Ä¢ Vectors, a collection of three numbers representing a position, movement, or direction in the world;
‚Ä¢ Booleans or "bools" for short, representing an abstract True or False;page.n101.n8â‚Ä¢ Entities, like myself, chickens, and minecarts;
‚Ä¢ Influences, iotas that represent various abstract ideas such as the absence of data ({#8d6acc|Null}) or complete nonsense ({#8d6acc|Garbage});
‚Ä¢ Patterns themselves, used for crafting magic items and truly mind-boggling feats like {i|spells that cast other spells};
‚Ä¢ A list of several of the above, gathered into a single iota.page.n101.n9œOf course, there's no such thing as a free lunch. All spells, and certain other actions, require {#74b3f2|media} as payment.

The best I can figure, a {#b38ef3|Hex} is a little bit like a plan of action presented to Nature-- in this analogy, the {#74b3f2|media} is used to provide the arguments to back it up, so Nature will accept your plan and carry it out.page.n101.n10≠That aside, it doesn't seem like anyone has done much research on exactly how {i|much} any particular piece of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|amethyst}} is valued. The best I can tell, an {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Shard}} is worth about five pieces of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}, and a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst Crystal}} is worth about ten.

Strangely enough, it seems like no other form of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|amethyst}} is suitable to be used in the casting of a {#b38ef3|Hex}. I suspect that whole blocks or crystals are too solid to be easily unraveled into {#74b3f2|media}.page.n101.n11ÕIt's also worth noting that each action will consume the {#74b3f2|media} it needs immediately, rather than all at once when the Hex finishes. Also, an action will always consume entire items-- an action that only requires one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}'s worth of {#74b3f2|media} will consume an entire {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst Crystal}}, if that's all that's present in my inventory.

Thus, it might be a good idea to bring dust for spellcasting too-- waste not, want not...page.n101.n12œI should also be careful to make sure I actually have enough Amethyst in my inventory-- some old texts say that Nature is happy to use one's own mind as payment instead. They describe the feeling as awful but strangely euphoric, "[...] an effervescent dissolution into light and energy..." Perhaps that's why all the old practitioners of the art went mad. I can't imagine burning pieces of my mind for power is {i|healthy}.page.n101.n13ÁMaybe something's changed, though. In my experiments, I've never managed to do it; if I run out of {#74b3f2|media}, the spell will simply fail to cast, as if some barrier is blocking it from harming me.

It would be interesting to get to the bottom of that mystery, but for now I suppose it'll keep me safe.page.n101.n14ÈI have also found an amusing tidbit on why so many practitioners of magic in general seem to go mad, which I may like as some light and flavorful reading not canonical to my world.

{i|Content Warning: some body horror and suggestive elements.}page.n101.n15ó	Finally, it seems spells have a maximum range of influence, about 32 blocks from my position. Trying to affect anything outside of that will cause the spell to fail.

Despite this, if I have a player's reference, I can affect them from anywhere. This only applies to affecting them directly, though; I cannot use this to affect the world around them if they're outside of my range.
I ought to be careful when giving out a reference like that. While friendly {#b38ef3|Hexcasters} could use them to great effect and utility, I shudder to think of what someone malicious might do with this.entry.n101Hex Casting 101page.mishaps.n1ÔUnfortunately, I am not (yet) a perfect being. I make mistakes from time to time in my study and casting of {#b38ef3|Hexes}; for example, misdrawing a pattern, or trying to an invoke an action with the wrong iotas. And Nature usually doesn't look too kindly on my mistakes-- causing what is called a {i|mishap}.page.mishaps.n2ÔA pattern that causes a mishap will glow red in my grid. Depending on the type of mistake, I can also expect a certain deleterious effect and a spray of red and colorful sparks as the mishandled {#74b3f2|media} curdles into light of a given color.page.mishaps.n3∑Fortunately, although the bad effects of mishaps are certainly {i|annoying}, none of them are especially destructive in the long term. Nothing better to do than dust myself off and try again ... but I should strive for better anyways.

Following is a list of mishaps I have compiled.9page.mishaps.invalid_patternïThe pattern drawn is not associated with any action.

Causes yellow sparks, and a {#8d6acc|garbage} will be pushed to the top of my stack.Epage.mishaps.invalid_pattern.titleInvalid Pattern;page.mishaps.not_enough_iotasÁThe action required more iotas than were on the stack.

Causes light gray sparks, and as many {#8d6acc|Garbages} as would be required to fill up the argument count will be pushed.Gpage.mishaps.not_enough_iotas.title!Not Enough Iotas/page.mishaps.stack_sizeøI tried to cast a spell which exceeded the size limit of the stack.

Causes black sparks, and my entire stack will be replaced with a single {#8d6acc|garbage}.;page.mishaps.stack_size.titleToo Many Iotas7page.mishaps.incorrect_iotaﬂThe action that was executed expected an iota of a certain type for an argument, but it got something invalid. If multiple iotas are invalid, the error message will only tell me about the error deepest in the stack.

Causes dark gray sparks, and the invalid iota will be replaced with {#8d6acc|garbage}.Cpage.mishaps.incorrect_iota.titleIncorrect IotaApage.mishaps.vector_out_of_rangeÒThe action tried to affect the world at a point that was out of my range.

Causes magenta sparks, and the items in my hands will be yanked out and flung towards the offending location.Mpage.mishaps.vector_out_of_range.title'Vector Out of AmbitApage.mishaps.entity_out_of_range—The action tried to affect an entity that was out of my range.

Causes pink sparks, and the items in my hands will be yanked out and flung towards the offending entity.Mpage.mishaps.entity_out_of_range.title'Entity Out of Ambit5page.mishaps.entity_immuneŸThe action tried to affect an entity that cannot be altered by it.

Causes blue sparks, and the items in my hands will be yanked out and flung towards the offending entity.Apage.mishaps.entity_immune.title!Entity is Immune/page.mishaps.math_errorÎThe action did something offensive to the laws of mathematics, such as dividing by zero.

Causes red sparks, pushes a {#8d6acc|garbage} to my stack, and my mind will be ablated, stealing half the vigor I have remaining. It seems that Nature takes offense to such operations, and divides {i|me} in retaliation.;page.mishaps.math_error.title%Mathematical Error7page.mishaps.incorrect_item≈The action requires some sort of item, but the item I supplied was not suitable.

Causes brown sparks. If the offending item was in my hand, it will be flung to the floor. If it was in entity form, it will be flung in the air.Cpage.mishaps.incorrect_item.titleIncorrect Item9page.mishaps.incorrect_block∑The action requires some sort of block at a target location, but the block supplied was not suitable.

Causes bright green sparks, and causes an ephemeral explosion at the given location. The explosion doesn't seem to harm me, the world, or anything else though; it's just startling.Epage.mishaps.incorrect_block.titleIncorrect Block3page.mishaps.needs_parensÛI attempted to draw a pattern that only works while {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|introspecting}} without first drawing {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Introspection}}.

Causes orange sparks, and pushes the pattern I tried to draw to the stack as an iota.?page.mishaps.needs_parens.title)Absent Introspection=page.mishaps.too_many_patternsáI attempted to evaluate too many patterns in one {#b38ef3|Hex}. Often, this happens because I've accidentally created an infinite loop.

Causes dark blue sparks, and chokes all the air out of me.Ipage.mishaps.too_many_patterns.titleLost in Thought-page.mishaps.true_nameïI attempted to {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|save a reference}} to another player to a permanent medium.

Causes black sparks, and robs me of my sight for approximately one minute.9page.mishaps.true_name.title!Transgress Other+page.mishaps.disabled«I tried to cast an action that has been disallowed by a server administrator.

Causes black sparks.7page.mishaps.disabled.title#Disallowed Action%page.mishaps.other˝A bug in the mod caused an iota of an invalid type or otherwise caused the spell to crash. {link=https://github.com/gamma-delta/HexMod/issues|Please open a bug report!}

Causes black sparks.1page.mishaps.other.title)Catastrophic Failureentry.mishapsMishaps!page.mishaps2.n1•I have discovered new and horrifying modes of failure. I must not succumb to them.5page.mishaps2.bad_mindflayüAttempted to flay the mind of something that I have either already used, or of a character not suitable for the target block.

Causes dark green sparks. Subjects I have already used will be killed outright, while those that are simply unsuitable will be slightly damaged.Apage.mishaps2.bad_mindflay.titleInert Mindflay/page.mishaps2.no_circleôTried to cast an action requiring a spell circle without a spell circle.

Causes light blue sparks, and upends my inventory onto the ground.;page.mishaps2.no_circle.title#Lack Spell Circle/page.mishaps2.no_record˜Tried to access an {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/akashiclib|{accent|Akashic Record}} at a location where there isn't one.

Causes purple sparks, and steals away some of my experience.;page.mishaps2.no_record.title'Lack Akashic Record5page.mishaps2.bad_shepherdüTried to activate a {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/directrix|{accent|Shepherd Directrix}} without a boolean on the top of the stack.

Causes red and white sparks, and forcibly ejects the Shepherd Directrix from the circle. This doesn't destroy it, thankfully, but I should make sure to go pick it up and replace it where it belongs.Apage.mishaps2.bad_shepherd.title)Improper Shepherdingentry.mishaps2'Enlightened Mishapspage.naming.n1˘The names given to actions by the ancients were certainly peculiar, but I think there's a certain kind of logic to them.

There seem to be certain groups of actions with common names, named for the number of iotas they remove from and add to the stack.page.naming.n2µ‚Ä¢ A {#8d6acc|Reflection} pops nothing and pushes one iota.
‚Ä¢ A {#8d6acc|Purification} pops one and pushes one.
‚Ä¢ A {#8d6acc|Distillation} pops two and pushes one.
‚Ä¢ An {#8d6acc|Exaltation} pops three or more and pushes one.
‚Ä¢ A {#8d6acc|Decomposition} pops one argument and pushes two.
‚Ä¢ A {#8d6acc|Disintegration} pops one and pushes three or more.
‚Ä¢ Finally, a {#8d6acc|Gambit} pushes or pops some other number (or rearranges the stack in some other manner).page.naming.n3ﬂSpells seem to be exempt from this nomenclature and are more or less named after what they do-- after all, why call it a {#fc77be|Demoman's Gambit} when you could just say {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/basic|{#fc77be|Explosion}}?entry.namingNaming Actionspage.stack.n1ôA {#8d6acc|Stack}, also known as a "LIFO", is a concept borrowed from computer science. In short, it's a collection of things designed so that you can only interact with the most recently used thing.

Think of a stack of plates, where new plates are added to the top: if you want to interact with a plate halfway down the stack, you have to remove the plates above it in order to get ahold of it.page.stack.n2•Because a stack is so simple, there's only so many things you can do with it:
‚Ä¢ {i|Adding something to it}, known formally as pushing,
‚Ä¢ {i|Removing the last added element}, known as popping, or
‚Ä¢ {i|Examining or modifying the last added element}, known as peeking.
We call the last-added element the "top" of the stack, in accordance with the dinner plate analogy.

As an example, if we push 1 to a stack, then push 2, then pop, the top of the stack is now 1.page.stack.n3œActions are (on the most part) restricted to interacting with the casting stack in these ways. They will pop some iotas they're interested in (known as "arguments" or "parameters"), process them, and push some number of results.

Of course, some actions (e.g. {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Mind's Reflection}}) might pop no arguments, and some actions (particularly spells) might push nothing afterwards.page.stack.n4ÀEven more complicated actions can be expressed in terms of pushing, popping, and peeking. For example, {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/stackmanip|{#fc77be|Jester's Gambit}} swaps the top two items of the stack. This can be thought of as popping two items and pushing them in opposite order. For another, {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/stackmanip|{#fc77be|Gemini Decomposition}} duplicates the top of the stack-- in other words, it peeks the stack and pushes a copy of what it finds.entry.stackThe Stackpage.vectors.n1„It seems I will need to be adroit with vectors if I am to get anywhere in my studies. A vector is a set of three numbers that can be used to represent a variety of things, usually either a position in the world, an offset from one position to another, or a direction in space. The following pages provide a basic summary of how vectors work and what I can do with them.page.vectors.n2„All the vectors I will be working with as a {#b38ef3|Hexcaster} are three-dimensional, since the world I inhabit has three dimensions.

Each vector has an X component, a Y component, and a Z component in that order, corresponding to the three coordinate axes of the world: the X axis runs east and west, the Y axis runs up and down, and the Z axis runs north and south.page.vectors.n3ÂThe most basic way to use a vector is as a {i|position}, representing a fixed point in the world - it could be the location of a block, or an entity, or perhaps some empty space that I wish to target with a spell.

A position vector is effectively just a set of coordinates pinpointing the exact location to which it refers. However, not all vectors represent positions.page.vectors.n4˚If I have a vector for the position of a block, and I want to target the block above it, I need to increase the Y component of the vector by 1. I can do this by taking a second vector with the value (0, 1, 0) and adding it to the first one. Likewise, if I wanted to target something two blocks to the east, I could add a second vector with the value (2, 0, 0). Both of these new vectors represent {i|offsets} from one position to a new position.page.vectors.n5∑Note that while position vectors and offset vectors represent different things, there is no fundamental difference between them - all vectors are just groups of numbers. In fact, I could even consider all my vectors to be one type or the other if I so desired: positions are just offsets from the world origin to the specified coordinates, and offsets are just positions relative to an arbitrary starting point.page.vectors.n6◊Two important properties of vectors are {i|magnitude} (length) and {i|direction}. These may be easiest to imagine when thinking of vectors as offsets.

If I were to choose a starting point, and then move in a straight line based on an offset vector, the distance I travelled would be the length of the vector, and the direction I travelled would be its direction.page.vectors.n7›There many ways to manipulate vectors. As described earlier, I can add them together, which adds the corresponding X, Y, and Z components of each vector. The same applies with subtraction - conveniently, subtracting one position vector from another results in the offset from the first position to the second.

Multiplying and dividing two vectors is not so simple; I can find more information on that topic {link=https://www.mathsisfun.com/algebra/vectors-dot-product.html|here} and {link=https://www.mathsisfun.com/algebra/vectors-cross-product.html|here}.page.vectors.n8ãI can also multiply or divide a vector by a single number to {i|scale} it. This multiplies or divides all three components of the vector by that number.

So long as the number is positive, this will multiply or divide the length of the vector without affecting its direction. If the number is negative, the length will be multiplied or divided as usual, but the direction will be reversed.page.vectors.n9¡Dividing a vector by its own length produces a unit vector, which has the same direction as the original but a length of exactly 1. These can be useful if the direction of the vector is all that matters. They are also useful in that I can multiply a unit vector by some number to get a new vector with exactly that length.

Many patterns that calculate a direction of some kind, such as {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|Alidade's Purification}, provide it as a unit vector.!page.vectors.n10€All the operations described thus far, and many more, can be performed using patterns found in the {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/math|Mathematics} section.

I can create new vectors by gathering information from entities using {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|Basic Patterns}, by using {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|Constants} to create unit vectors for basic directions, or by directly combining three numbers with {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/math|Vector Exaltation}.!page.vectors.n11ÎTo understand more advanced concepts related to vectors, I may want to do further research outside the bounds of this notebook. The video below may also serve as a useful reference.entry.vectors'A Primer On Vectors'page.whats_a_hex.n1ÀConceptually, a {#b38ef3|Hex} is exactly as described in {link=@entry:hexcasting:casting/101|Hex Casting 101}: a sequence of actions, usually done with the goal of performing some kind of effect in the world by means of one or more spells.

This idea of a {#b38ef3|Hex} is fine for on-the-fly casting, but if I wish to record my work for posterity (or simply for my own reuse) I may need something a little more concrete.'page.whats_a_hex.n2«
Typically, this takes the form of a list of pattern iotas, as created by the techniques described in {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|Escaping Patterns}. With my {#b38ef3|Hex} placed onto the stack this way, I can cast it using meta-evaluation patterns like {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/meta|Hermes' Gambit}, or infuse it into a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|Casting Item} item for easy reuse.

Note that this list is no different from any other list iota - I can manipulate it, save it into {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/thought_knot|permanent} {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|storage}, or even incorporate it into another {#b38ef3|Hex}.'page.whats_a_hex.n3˘Note also that this list need not be composed exclusively of pattern iotas. Inserting iotas of other types into my {#b38ef3|Hexes} won't normally end well, but {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|certain techniques} allow me to do it safely. Furthermore, some {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/meta|special iotas} can be directly evaluated just like a pattern.

With this in mind, {i|any} list of iotas can in theory be a {#b38ef3|Hex} - the only requirement is that I can successfully cast it.#entry.whats_a_hexWhat is a Hex?%page.akashiclib.n1ìI KNOW SO MUCH it is ONLY RIGHT to have a place to store it all. Information can be stored in books but it is oh so so so so {i|slow} to write by hand and read by eye. I demand BETTER. And so I shall MAKE better.

... I am getting worse ... do not know if I have time to write everything bursting through my head before expiring.%page.akashiclib.n2•	The library. Here. My plans.

Like how patterns are associated with actions, I can associate my own patterns with iotas in any way I choose. An {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/akashiclib|{accent|Akashic Record}} controls the library, and each {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/akashiclib|{accent|Akashic Bookshelf}} stores one pattern mapped to one iota. These must all be directly connected together, touching, within 128 blocks. An {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/akashiclib|{accent|Akashic Ligature}} doesn't do anything but count as a connecting block, to extend the size of my library.=page.akashiclib.akashic_record…Allocating and assigning patterns is simple but oh so boring. I have better things to do. I will need a mind well-used to its work for the extraction to stay sound.%page.akashiclib.n3ôThen to operate the library is simple, the patterns are routed through the librarian and it looks them up and returns the iota to you. Two actions do the work. {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/akashic_patterns|Notes here}.

Using an empty {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/scroll|{accent|scroll}} on a bookshelf copies the pattern there onto the {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/scroll|{accent|scroll}}. Sneaking and using an empty hand clears the datum in the shelf.!entry.akashiclib#Akashic Libraries+page.brainsweeping.n1ôA secret was revealed to me. I saw it. I cannot forget its horror. The idea skitters across my brain.

I believed-- oh, foolishly, I {i|believed} --that {#74b3f2|Media} is the spare energy left over by thought. But now I {i|know} what it is: the energy {i|of} thought.+page.brainsweeping.n2ˇIt is produced by thinking sentience and allows sentience to think. It is a knot tying that braids into its own string. The Entity I naively anthromorphized as Nature is simply a grand such tangle, or perhaps the set of all tangles, or ... if I think it hurts I have so many synapses and all of them can think pain at once ALL OF THEM CAN SEE

I am not holding on. My notes. Quickly.+page.brainsweeping.n3ΩThe villagers of this world have enough consciousness left to be extracted. Place it into a block, warp it, change it. Intricate patterns caused by different patterns of thought, the abstract neural pathways of their jobs and lives mapped into the cold physic of solid atoms.

This is what {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/great_spells/brainsweep|{#fc77be|Flay Mind}} does, the extraction. Target the villager entity and the destination block. Ten {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst}} for this perversion of will.Gpage.brainsweeping.budding_amethyst°And an application. For this flaying, any sort of villager will do, if it has developed enough. Other recipes require more specific types. NO MORE must I descend into the hellish earth for my {#74b3f2|media}.'entry.brainsweeping/On The Flaying of Minds#page.directrix.n1˘Simpler than the task of creating a self-sustaining wave of {#74b3f2|media} is the task of directing it. Ordinarily the wave disintegrates when coming upon a crossroads, but with a mind to guide it, an exit direction can be controlled.

This manipulation is not nearly so fine as the delicacy of actuating a spell circle. In fact, it might be possible to do it by hand... but the packaged minds I have access to now would be so very convenient.#page.directrix.n2ÒA {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/directrix|{accent|Directrix}} accepts a wave of {#74b3f2|media} and determines to which of the arrows it will exit from, depending on the villager mind inside.

I am not certain if this idea was bestowed upon me, or if my mind is bent around the barrier enough to splint off its own ideas now... but if the idea came from my own mind, if I thought it, can it be said it was bestowed? The brain is a vessel for the mind and the mind is a vessel for ideas and the ideas vessel thought and thought sees all and knows all-- I MUST N O T=page.directrix.empty_directrixèFirstly, a design for the cradle ... although, perhaps "substrate" would be more accurate a word. Without a mind guiding it, the output direction is determined by microscopic fluctuations in the {#74b3f2|media} wave and surroundings, making it effectively random.Cpage.directrix.directrix_redstone’A {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/directrix|{accent|Mason Directrix}} switches output side based on a redstone signal. Without a signal, the exit is the {#74b3f2|media}-color side; with a signal, the exit is the redstone-color side.Apage.directrix.directrix_boolean±A {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/directrix|{accent|Shepherd Directrix}} switches output side based on a boolean popped from the stack. A {#8d6acc|True} makes the wave exit from the back, a {#8d6acc|False} from the front. Failing to provide a boolean at all will cause a mishap.entry.directrixDirectrices/page.fanciful_staves.n1çIt is only right as I shed the husk of ignorance I replace my tools, my palm-polished staves. These new constructions of mine have no additional properties -- but they are so glorious, oh so Glorious... They match the radiance winking at the corners of my sight.+entry.fanciful_stavesFanciful Stavespage.impetus.n1—The fluctuation of {#74b3f2|media} required to actuate a spell circle is complex. Even the mortal with sharpest eyes and steadiest hands could not serve as an {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Impetus}} and weave {#74b3f2|media} into the self-sustaining oroboros required.

The problem is that the mind is too full of other useless {i|garbage}.page.impetus.n2’At a ... metaphysical level-- I must be careful with these thoughts, I cannot lose myself, I have become too valuable --moving {#74b3f2|media} moves the mind, and the mind must be moved for the process to work. But, the mind is simply too {i|heavy} with other thoughts to move nimbly enough.

It is like an artisan trying to repair a watch while wearing mittens.page.impetus.n3ÛThere are several solutions to this conundrum: through meditative techniques one can learn to blank the mind, although I am not certain a mind free enough to actuate a circle can concentrate hard enough to do the motions.

Certain unsavory compounds can create a similar effect, but I know nothing of them and do not plan to learn. I must not rely on the chemicals of my brain.page.impetus.n4üThe solution I aim for, then, is to specialize a mind. Remove it from the tyranny of nerves, clip all outputs but delicate splays of {#74b3f2|media}-manipulating apparati, cauterize all inputs but the signal to start its work.

The process of {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/brainsweeping|{#fc77be|mindflaying}} I am now familiar with will do excellently; the mind of a villager is complex enough to do the work, but not so complex as to resist its reformation.5page.impetus.empty_impetus≠First, the cradle. Although it does not work as an {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Impetus}}, the flow of {#74b3f2|media} in a circle will only exit out the side pointed to by the arrows. This allows me to change the plane in which the wave flows, for example.?page.impetus.impetus_rightclick≠Then, to transpose the mind. Villagers of different professions will lend different actuation conditions to the resulting {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Impetus}}. A {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Toolsmith Impetus}} activates on a simple .Ipage.impetus.impetus_storedplayer.n1ëA {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Cleric Impetus}} activates when receiving a redstone signal. It may be bound to a player by using an item with a reference to that player, like a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|{accent|Focus}}, on the block.Ipage.impetus.impetus_storedplayer.n2°Peculiarly to this {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Impetus}}, the bound player, as well as a small region around them, are always accessible to the spell circle. It's as if they were standing within the bounds of the circle, no matter how far away they might stand.

The bound player is shown when looking at a {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Cleric Impetus}} through a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/lens|{accent|Scrying Lens}}.3page.impetus.impetus_look„A {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Fletcher Impetus}} activates when looked at for a short time.entry.impetusImpeti1page.quenching_allays.n1ìTHEY ARE BITS OF MEDIA. How did I not see it sooner? They are -- as I am a heap of flesh with a scrap, blessed with a scrap of thought, an Allay is a self-sustaining quarrel of media pinned to a scrap of flesh. It explains everything -- their propensity for media, their response to music, I SEE NOW, HOW did the ones before NOT?1page.quenching_allays.n2€And given this it is only RIGHT I conquer their peculiar minds -- their peculiar selves -- that is all they are, a mind, a self, a coda. Something about their phase speaks to me. I can... I can compress {#74b3f2|media} with them, overlay two wends of thought in one space, physical and cognitive, all and once.

Somehow, the process produces {#74b3f2|media} of its own. How? Perhaps -- perhaps MY work, the process of doing it --1page.quenching_allays.n3›It matters not. I matter not. They matter not, all that matters is what it does. And this is it.

It must hurt so very much.

Ten {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} is the price to enact such a perverse ritual.1page.quenching_allays.n4çThe product is fragile. Breaking it shatters it into pieces, with {#8d6acc|Fortune} increasing the yield... if I wish the block itself I need a silken touch.

The produced shards are worth thrice an {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst Crystal}} apiece. The block itself is worth four of the shards.1page.quenching_allays.n5•They are mercurial, they seem to twist and wink under my fingers, and by giving them a mentor in another form of {#74b3f2|media} they may be coerced into its shape, in an equivalent exchange of {#74b3f2|media}.-entry.quenching_allays!Quenching Allays)page.spellcircles.n1€I KNOW what the {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/slate|{accent|slates}} are for. The grand assemblies lost to time. The patterns scribed on them can be actuated in sequence, automatically. Thought and power ricocheting through, one by one by one by one by one by through and through and THROUGH AND -- I must not I must not I should know better than to think that way.)page.spellcircles.n2ÒTo start the ritual I need an {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Impetus}} to create a self-sustaining wave of {#74b3f2|media}. That wave travels along a track of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/slate|{accent|slates}} or other suitable blocks, casting any patterns it finds.

If there is no way for the wave to return to the {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Impetus}}, the casting will fail immediately. Also, the direction the wave exits any given block MUST be unambiguous, or the casting will fail at the block with too many neighbors.)page.spellcircles.n3ÉAs a result, the outline of the spell "circle" may be any closed shape, concave or convex, and it may face any direction. In fact, with the application of certain other blocks it is possible to make a spell circle that spans all three dimensions. I doubt such an oddity has very much use, but I must allocate myself a bit of vapid levity to encourage my crude mind to continue my work.)page.spellcircles.n4∑Miracle of miracles, the circle will withdraw {#74b3f2|media} neither from my inventory nor my mind. Instead, crystallized shards of {#74b3f2|media} must be provided to the {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Impetus}} via hopper, or other such artifice.

The application of a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/lens|{accent|Scrying Lens}} will show how much {#74b3f2|media} is inside an {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Impetus}}, in units of dust.)page.spellcircles.n5ÉHowever, a spell cast from a circle does have one major limitation: it is unable to affect anything outside of the circle's bounds. That is, it cannot interact with anything outside of the cuboid of minimum size which encloses every block composing it (so a concave spell circle can still affect things in the concavity).)page.spellcircles.n6°There is also a limit on the number of blocks the wave can travel through before it disintegrates, but it is large enough I doubt I will have any trouble.

Conversely, there are some actions that can only be cast from a circle. Fortunately, none of them are spells; they all seem to deal with components of the circle itself. My notes on the subject are {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/circle|here}.)page.spellcircles.n7˘I also found a sketch of a spell circle used by the ancients buried in my notes. Facing this page is my (admittedly poor) copy of it.

The patterns there would have been executed counter-clockwise, starting with {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Mind's Reflection}} and ending with {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/great_spells/teleport|{#fc77be|Greater Teleport}}.Opage.spellcircles.teleport_circle.title)Teleportation Circle%entry.spellcirclesSpell Circles!page.the_work.n1©I have seen so many things. Unspeakable things. Innumerable things. I could write three words and turn my mind inside-out and smear my brains across the shadowed walls of my skull to decay into fluff and nothing.!page.the_work.n2˚I have seen staccato-needle patterns and acid-etched schematics written on the inside of my eyelids. They smolder there-- they dance, they taunt, they {i|ache}. I'm possessed by an intense {i|need} to draw them, create them. Form them. Liberate them from the gluey shackles of my mortal mind-- present them in their Glory to the world for all to see.

All shall see.

All will see.entry.the_workThe Workpage.interop.n1ôThe art of {#b38ef3|Hexcasting} is versatile. If I find that my world has been {i|modified} by certain other powers, it's possible that I may use {#b38ef3|Hexcasting} in harmony and combination with them.page.interop.n2”I should keep in mind, however, that Nature seems to have paid less attention in crafting these aspects of my art; strange behavior and bugs are to be expected. I'm sure the mod developer will do her best to correct them, but I must remember this is a less important pastime to her.

I may also find that there are sharp disregards to balance in the costs and effects of the interoperating powers. In such a case I suppose I will have to be responsible and restrain myself from using them.page.interop.n3õFinally, if I find myself interested in the lore and stories of this world, I do not think any notes compiled while examining these interoperations should be considered as anything more than light trifles.entry.interop+Cross-Mod Interations-page.interop.pehkui.n1ÛI have discovered methods of changing the size of entities, and querying how much larger or smaller they are than normal./page.interop.pehkui.getÀGet the scale of the entity, as a proportion of their normal size. For most entities, this will be 1.Iaction.hexcasting.interop.pehkui.get/Gulliver's Purification/page.interop.pehkui.set„Set the scale of the entity, passing in a proportion of their normal size. Costs about 1 {accent|Amethyst Shard}.Iaction.hexcasting.interop.pehkui.setAlter Scale)entry.interop.pehkuiPehkuipage.abacus.n1∑	Although there are {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/numbers|{#fc77be|patterns for drawing numbers}}, I find them ... cumbersome, to say the least.

Fortunately, the old masters of my craft invented an ingenious device called an {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/abacus|{accent|Abacus}} to provide numbers to my casting. I simply set the number to what I want, then read the value using {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Scribe's Reflection}}, just like I would read a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/thought_knot|{accent|Thought-Knot}} or {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|{accent|Focus}}.page.abacus.n2£To operate one, I simply hold it, sneak, and scroll. If in my main hand, the number will increment or decrement by 1, or 10 if I am also holding {key|sprint}. If in my off hand, the number will increment or decrement by 0.1, or 0.001 if I am also holding {key|sprint}.

I can shake the abacus to reset it to zero by sneak-right-clicking.3page.abacus.crafting.descK{i|Mathematics? That's for eggheads!}entry.abacusAbacus%page.amethyst.dustπIt seems that I'll find three different forms of amethyst when breaking a crystal inside a geode. The smallest denomination seems to be a small pile of shimmering dust, worth a relatively small amount of {#74b3f2|media}.'page.amethyst.shard´The second is a whole shard of amethyst, of the type non-{#b38ef3|Hexcasters} might be used to. This has about as much {#74b3f2|media} inside as five {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.+page.amethyst.crystalëFinally, I'll rarely find a large crystal crackling with energy. This has about as much {#74b3f2|media} inside as ten units of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} (or two {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Shards}}).%page.amethyst.lore„{i|The old man sighed and raised a hand toward the fire. He unlocked a part of his brain that held the memories of the mountains around them. He pulled the energies from those lands, as he learned to do in Terisia City with Drafna, Hurkyl, the archimandrite, and the other mages of the Ivory Towers. He concentrated, and the flames writhed as they rose from the logs, twisting upon themselves until they finally formed a soft smile.}entry.amethystAmethyst/page.ancient_cyphers.n1πWhile adventuring, I've come across what appear to be {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Cyphers}} created by the Hexcasters of the ancient past. These {accent|Ancient Cyphers} work just like the ones I can craft: they can't be recharged, and will break when their reserve of {#74b3f2|Media} runs out. However, unlike my own {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Cyphers}}, their decayed structure allows me to glimpse the {#b38ef3|Hex} stored within./page.ancient_cyphers.n2ÖThese may be useful for learning new techniques - who better to teach me than ancient masters of the art?

Alternatively, I could erase the contents of one and store my own {#b38ef3|Hex} inside, creating a casting item that clearly displays its own function./page.ancient_cyphers.n3≠The {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/pigments|{accent|Pigment}} baked into these relics is a unique blend of purple and orange, matching their copper-and-amethyst appearance. I can replicate it using the above recipe.+entry.ancient_cyphersAncient Cyphers-page.creative_items.n1≥I have invented some fanciful items that can only be acquired through {i|creative} means.Kpage.creative_items.creative_unlocker´The {#74b3f2|Media Cube} is a near-limitless source of power, acting as an infinite source of {#74b3f2|media} for whatever {#b38ef3|Hexes} I wish to cast. It also looks quite tasty. Eating it might perhaps reveal more about the world, possibly delving into spoiler territory...-page.creative_items.n2¡Additionally, it seems that by renaming a {#74b3f2|Media Cube} at an Anvil, I should be able to utilize it to test and understand more about my {#b38ef3|Hexes}. If I have somehow acquired Creative power over the world, I might wish to turn that off before attempting any of the following.Ipage.creative_items.debug_media.descçBy renaming the {#74b3f2|Media Cube} to 'debug media', I will be given feedback in the chat window about the amount of media consumed.Kpage.creative_items.debug_media.titledebug media)entry.creative_items'Creative-Only Items%page.decoration.n1ÀIn the course of my studies I have discovered some building blocks and trifles that I may find aesthetically pleasing. I've compiled the methods of making them here.Ypage.decoration.ancient_scroll.crafting.descÎBrown dye works well enough to simulate the look of an {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/scroll|{accent|ancient scroll}}.Gpage.decoration.tiles.crafting.desc£{link=@entry:hexcasting:items/decoration|{accent|Amethyst Tiles}} can also be made in a Stonecutter.

{link=@entry:hexcasting:items/decoration|{accent|Blocks of Amethyst Dust}} (next page) will fall like sand.Ipage.decoration.sconce.crafting.descÖ{link=@entry:hexcasting:items/decoration|{accent|Amethyst Sconces}} emit light and particles, as well as a pleasing chiming sound.!entry.decoration#Decorative Blockspage.edified.n1„By infusing {#74b3f2|media} into a sapling via the use of {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/blockworks|{#fc77be|Edify Sapling}}, I can create what is called an {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/edified|{#8d6acc|Edified Tree}}. They tend to be tall and pointy, with ridged bark and wood that grows in a strange spiral pattern. Their leaves come in three pretty colors.page.edified.n2˝I would assume the wood would have some properties relevant to {#b38ef3|Hexcasting}. But, if it does, I cannot seem to find them. For all intents and purposes it appears to be just wood, albeit of a very strange color.

I suppose for now I will use it for decoration; the full suite of standard wood blocks can be crafted from them.

Of course, I can strip them with an axe as well.5page.edified.crafting.desc´{i|Their smooth trunks, with white bark, gave the effect of enormous columns sustaining the weight of an immense foliage, full of shade and silence.}entry.edifiedEdified Treespage.focus.n1çA {accent|Focus} is like a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/thought_knot|{accent|Thought-Knot}}, in that iota can be written to or read from it. However, the advantage of a focus is that it is {i|reusable}. If I make a mistake in the iota I write to a {accent|Focus}, I can simply cast {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Scribe's Gambit}} again and write over the iota inside.page.focus.n2ΩIf I wish to protect a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|{accent|focus}} from accidentally being overwritten, I can seal it with wax by crafting it with a {accent|Honeycomb}. Attempting to use {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Scribe's Gambit}} on a sealed focus will fail.

{link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/hexcasting|{#fc77be|Erase Item}} will remove this seal along with the contents.page.focus.n3çIndeed, the only advantage of my {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/thought_knot|{accent|Thought-Knot}}s have over {accent|Foci} is that {accent|Foci} are more expensive to produce. My research indicates that the early practitioners of the art used exclusively {accent|Foci}, with the {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/thought_knot|{accent|Thought-Knot}} being an original creation of mine.

Whoever those ancient people were, they must have been very prosperous.1page.focus.crafting.descA{i|Poison apples, poison worms.}entry.focusFocus%page.hexcasting.n1ÛAlthough the flexibility of casting {#b38ef3|Hexes} "on the go" with my {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|Staff}} is quite helpful, it's a huge pain to have to wave it around repeatedly just to accomplish a basic task. If I could save a common {#b38ef3|Hex} for later reuse, it would simplify things a lot-- and allow me to share my {#b38ef3|Hexes} with friends, too.%page.hexcasting.n2ÎTo do this, I can craft one of three types of magic items: {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Cyphers}}, {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Trinkets}}, or {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Artifacts}}. All of them hold the patterns of a given {#b38ef3|Hex} inside, along with a small battery containing {#74b3f2|media}.

Simply holding one and pressing will cast the patterns inside, as if the holder had cast them out of a staff, using its internal battery.%page.hexcasting.n3ïEach item has its own quirks:

{link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Cyphers}} are fragile, destroyed after their internal {#74b3f2|media} reserves are gone, and {i|cannot} be recharged;

{link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Trinkets}} can be cast as much as the holder likes, as long as there's enough {#74b3f2|media} left, but become useless afterwards until recharged;%page.hexcasting.n4π{link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Artifacts}} are the most powerful of all-- after their {#74b3f2|media} is depleted, they can use {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst}} from the holder's inventory to pay for the {#b38ef3|Hex}, just as I do when casting with a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|Staff}}. Of course, this also means the spell might consume their mind if there's not enough {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst}}.

Once I've made an empty magic item in a mundane crafting bench, I infuse the {#b38ef3|Hex} into it using (what else but) a spell appropriate to the item. {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/hexcasting|I've catalogued the patterns here.}%page.hexcasting.n5—Each infusion spell requires an entity and a {link=@entry:hexcasting:casting/whats_a_hex|list of iotas} to cast on the stack. The entity must be a {#74b3f2|media}-holding item entity (i.e. {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|amethyst}} dropped on the ground); the entity is consumed and forms the battery.

Usefully, it seems that the {#74b3f2|media} in the battery is not consumed in chunks as it is when casting with a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|Staff}}-- rather, the {#74b3f2|media} "melts down" into one continuous pool. Thus, if I store a {#b38ef3|Hex} that only costs one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}'s worth of media, a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Crystal}} used as the battery will allow me to cast it 10 times.;page.hexcasting.crafting.desc√{i|We have a saying in our field: "Magic isn't". It doesn't "just work," it doesn't respond to your thoughts, you can't throw fireballs or create a roast dinner from thin air or turn a bunch of muggers into frogs and snails.}!entry.hexcastingCasting Items-page.jeweler_hammer.n1›After being careless with the sources of my {#74b3f2|media} one too many times, I have devised a tool to work around my clumsiness.

Using the delicate nature of crystallized {#74b3f2|media} as a fixture for a pickaxe, I can create the {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/jeweler_hammer|{accent|Jeweler's Hammer}}. It acts like an {accent|Iron Pickaxe}, for the most part, but can't break anything that takes up an entire block's space.Cpage.jeweler_hammer.crafting.descá{i|Carefully, she cracked the half ruby, letting the spren escape.})entry.jeweler_hammer!Jeweler's Hammerpage.lens.n1è{#74b3f2|Media} can have peculiar effects on any type of information, in specific circumstances. Coating a glass in a thin film of it can lead to ... elucidating insights.

By holding a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/lens|{accent|Scrying Lens}} in my hand, certain blocks will display additional information when I look at them.page.lens.n2…For example, looking at a piece of {accent|Redstone} will display its signal strength. I suspect I will discover other blocks with additional insight as my studies into my art progress.

In addition, holding it while casting using a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|Staff}} will shrink the spacing between dots, allowing me to draw more on my grid.

I can also wear it on my head as a strange sort of monocle./page.lens.crafting.desck{i|You must learn... to see what you are looking at.}entry.lensScrying Lenspage.phials.n1ØI find it quite ... irritating, how Nature refuses to give me change for my work. If all I have on hand is {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst}}, even the tiniest {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Archer's Distillation}} will consume the entire crystal, wasting the remaining {#74b3f2|media}.

Fortunately, it seems I've found a way to somewhat allay this problem.page.phials.n2˜I've found old scrolls describing a {accent|Glass Bottle} infused with {#74b3f2|media}. When casting {#b38ef3|Hexes}, my spells would then draw {#74b3f2|media} out of the phial. The liquid form of the {#74b3f2|media} would let me take exact change, so to speak; nothing would be wasted. It's quite like the internal battery of a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Trinket}}, or similar; I can even {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/hexcasting|{#fc77be|Recharge}} them in the same manner.page.phials.n3ëUnfortunately, the art of actually {i|making} the things seems to have been lost to time. I've found a {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/great_spells/make_battery|{#8d6acc|hint at the pattern used to craft it}}, but the technique is irritatingly elusive, and I can't seem to do it successfully. I suspect I will figure it out with study and practice, though. For now, I will simply deal with the wasted {#74b3f2|media}...

But I won't settle for it forever.!page.phials.desc'{i|Drink the milk.}entry.phialsPhials of Media!page.pigments.n1’The old practitioners of my art sometimes identified themselves by a color, emblematic of them and their {#b38ef3|Hexes}. Although their names have faded, their colors remain. It seems a special kind of pigment, offered to Nature in the right way, would "[...] paint one's thoughts in a manner pleasing to Nature, inducing a miraculous change in personal color."!page.pigments.n2øI'm not certain on the specifics, but I believe I have isolated the formulae for many different colors and blends of pigments. To apply a pigment, I hold it in one hand and cast {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/colorize|{#fc77be|Internalize Pigment}} with the other; this consumes the pigment.

The pigments seem to affect the color of the sparks of {#74b3f2|media} emitted when I cast a {#b38ef3|Hex} and my {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/sentinels|{#8d6acc|sentinel}}, but I don't doubt that the color will show up elsewhere.Gpage.pigments.colored.crafting.descUPigments in all the colors of the rainbow.+page.pigments.specialŸAnd finally, a pair of special pigments. {accent|Soulglimmer Pigment} shines with colors wholly unique to me, and {accent|Vacant Pigment} restores my original amethyst hue.

{i|And all the colors I am inside have not been invented yet.}entry.pigmentsPigmentspage.potions.n1ªPeering through a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/lens|{accent|Scrying Lens}} allows me to fit more patterns onto my casting grid. But what if I want to improve my casting even further? Alchemy has an answer.

By adding a pinch of Amethyst Dust to an {accent|Awkward Potion}, I can create a brew that increases the size of my grid in much the same way as the Lens. I can even use both at once for the ultimate grid!page.potions.n2ÛThe Potion of Clarity can be extended, strengthened, and modified like any other potion.

It can also be corrupted with a {accent|Fermented Spider Eye} to produce a variant with the opposite effect. This may be useful for dealing with enemy casters.7page.potions.effects.header)Clarity and Cloudingentry.potionsHex Potionspage.scroll.n1ÈA {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/scroll|{accent|Scroll}} is a convenient method of sharing a pattern with others. I can copy a pattern onto one with {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Scribe's Gambit}}, after which it will display in a tooltip.

I can also place them on the wall as decoration or edification, like a painting, in sizes from 1x1 to 3x3 blocks. Using {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} on such a scroll will have it display the stroke order.page.scroll.n2ﬂIn addition, I can also find so-called {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/scroll|{accent|Ancient Scrolls}} in the dungeons and strongholds of the world. These contain the stroke order of {#8d6acc|Great Spells}, powerful magicks rumored to be too powerful for the hands and minds of mortals...

If those "mortals" couldn't cast them, I'm not sure they deserve to know them.3page.scroll.crafting.descﬂ{i|I write upon clean white parchment with a sharp quill and the blood of my students, divining their secrets.}entry.scrollScrollspage.slate.n1Ÿ{link=@entry:hexcasting:items/slate|{accent|Slates}} are similar to {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/scroll|{accent|Scrolls}}; I can copy a pattern to them and place them in the world to display the pattern.

However, I have read vague tales of grand assemblies of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/slate|{accent|Slates}}, used to cast {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/spellcircles|{#8d6acc|great rituals}} more powerful than can be handled by a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|Staff}}.page.slate.n2◊Perhaps this knowledge will be revealed to me with time. But for now, I suppose they make a quaint piece of decor.

At the least, they can be placed on any side of a block, unlike {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/scroll|{accent|Scrolls}}.1page.slate.crafting.descK{i|This is the letter "a." Learn it.}page.slate.n3ŸI'm also aware of other types of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/slate|{accent|Slates}}, slates that do not contain patterns but seem to be inlaid with other ... strange ... oddities. It hurts my brain to think about them, as if my thoughts get bent around their designs, following their pathways, bending and wefting through their labyrinthine depths, through and through and through channeled through and processed and--

... I almost lost myself. Maybe I should postpone my studies of those.entry.slateSlates#page.spellbook.n1°A {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/spellbook|{accent|Spellbook}} is the culmination of my art-- it acts like an entire library of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|{accent|Foci}}. Up to {#8d6acc|sixty-four} of them, to be exact.

Each page can hold a single iota, and I can select the active page (the page that iotas are saved to and copied from) by sneak-scrolling while holding it, or simply holding it in my off-hand and scrolling while casting a {#b38ef3|Hex}.#page.spellbook.n2Ö
Like a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|{accent|Focus}}, there exists a simple method to prevent accidental overwriting. Crafting it with a {accent|Honeycomb} will lacquer the current page, preventing {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Scribe's Gambit}} from modifying its contents. Also like a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|{accent|Focus}}, using {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/hexcasting|{#fc77be|Erase Item}} will remove the lacquer along with the page's contents.

I can also name each page individually in an anvil. Naming it will change only the name of the currently selected page, for easy browsing.9page.spellbook.crafting.desc’{i|Wizards love words. Most of them read a great deal, and indeed one strong sign of a potential wizard is the inability to get to sleep without reading something first.}entry.spellbookSpellbookpage.staff.n1•A {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|Staff}} is my entry point into casting all {#b38ef3|Hexes}, large and small. By holding it and pressing , I begin casting a {#b38ef3|Hex}; then I can click and drag to draw patterns.

It's little more than a chunk of {#74b3f2|media} on the end of a stick; that's all that's needed, after all.1page.staff.crafting.descg{i|Don't fight; flame, light; ignite; burn bright.}entry.staffStaves)page.thought_knot.n1ÈThe forgetful often tie a piece of string about their finger to help them remember something important. I believe this idea might be of use in my art. A specially knotted piece of string should be able to hold a single iota stably, irregardless of my stack.

I will call my invention a {accent|Thought-Knot}.)page.thought_knot.n2…	When I craft it, it stores no iota. Using {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Scribe's Gambit}} while holding a {accent|Thought-Knot} in my other hand will remove the top of the stack and save it into the {accent|Thought-Knot}. Using {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Scribe's Reflection}} will copy whatever iota's in the {accent|Thought-Knot} and add it to the stack.

Once a {accent|Thought-Knot} has been written to, the string is indelibly tangled; the iota can be read any number of times, but there is no way to erase or overwrite it. Fortunately, they are not expensive.)page.thought_knot.n3ãAlso, if I store an entity in a {accent|Thought-Knot} and try to recall it after the referenced entity has died or otherwise disappeared, the {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Scribe's Reflection}} will add {#8d6acc|Null} to the stack instead.?page.thought_knot.crafting.desc¡{i|How would you feel if someone saw you wearing a sign that said, "I am dashing and handsome?"}%entry.thought_knotThought-Knot-page.lore.cardamom1.n1ı{i|Full title: Letter from Cardamom Steles to Her Father, #1}

Dear Papa,
Every day it seems I have more reason to thank you for saving up to send me to the Grand Library. The amount I am learning is incredible! I feel I don't have the skill with words needed to express myself fully... it is wonderful to be here.-page.lore.cardamom1.n2ÂI sit in the main dome as I write this. It's maintained by the Hexcasting Corps; they have some sort of peculiar mechanism at the top that captures the stray thought energy as it leaks out from the desks and desks of hard-working students, as I understand it. One of my friends in the dormitory, Amanita, is studying the subject, and oh how she loves to explain it to me at length, although I confess I do not understand it very well.-page.lore.cardamom1.n3ç	The way I understand it, our processes of thought--the intangible mechanisms by which I move my pen and by which you read this letter--are not completely efficient. A small amount of that energy is released into the environment, like how a wagon's axle is hot to the touch after it has been turning for a while. This spare energy is called "media." One person's spare media is trifiling, but the hundreds of thinking people in the main dome have a sort of multiplicative effect, and combined with some sort of ingenious mechanism, it can be solidified into a sort of purple crystal.-page.lore.cardamom1.n4õBut that's enough about her studies. I returned from my first expedition with the Geology Corps today! My apologies for not sending a letter before I left; the date crept up on me. We ventured into a crack in the earth to the east of the Grand and spent the night camping under the rock and soil. We kept to well-lit and well-traveled areas of the cave, of course, and in all honesty it was likely safer in there than the night surface, but oh how I was scared!-page.lore.cardamom1.n5´Fortunately the night passed without mishap, and we proceeded deeper into the cave for our examination of the local veins of ore. We were looking for trace veins of a purple crystal called "amethyst," which supposedly occurs in small amounts deep in the rock. We did not find anything, sadly, and returned to the sunlit surface empty-handed.-page.lore.cardamom1.n6ıCome to think of it, the description of this "amethyst" I now realize closely matches those crystals of media Amanita speaks of. Imagine if these nuggets of thought occurred naturally under the ground! I can't imagine why that might happen, though...-page.lore.cardamom1.n7◊As a student, I am entitled to send one letter by Akashic post every three months, free of charge. Unfortunately, you know how thin my moneybags are ... so I am afraid this offer is the only method I may communicate with you. I will of course appreciate immensely if you manage to scrounge together the money to send a letter back, but it seems our communications may be limited. I hate to be cut off from you so, but the skills I gain here will be more than repayment. Imagine, I will be the first member of our family to be anything other than a farmer!-page.lore.cardamom1.n8•So, I suppose I will write again in three months' time.

Yours,
-- Cardamom Steles)entry.lore.cardamom1'Cardamom Steles, #1-page.lore.cardamom2.n1á{i|Full title: Letter from Cardamom Steles to Her Father, #2}

Dear Papa,
... Goodness, what an ordeal it is to try to summarize the last three months into a short letter. Such a cruel task set before me by this miracle I receive entirely for free! Woe is me.-page.lore.cardamom2.n2ÅMy studies with the Geology Corps have been progressing smoothly. We have gone on more expeditions, deeper into the earth, to where the smooth gray stone makes way to a hard, flaky slate. It creates such an awful, choking dust under your feet... it's incredible what hostility there is below all of our feet all the time, even disregarding the creatures of the dark. (I have had one or two encounters with them, but I know how you shudder to think of me having to fight for my life, so I will not write of them.)-page.lore.cardamom2.n3ßWe did manage to find some of this amethyst, however. There was a small vein with a few trace crystals on one of our expeditions. We were under strict instructions to keep none of them and turn them in to our Corps prefect immediately. I find the whole affair rather ridiculous; they treat it like some matter of enormous importance and secrecy, and yet have a group of a dozen students, all barely six months at the Grand Library, trying to excavate barely ten drams of the stuff with twelve prospector's picks in a square foot...-page.lore.cardamom2.n4—I cannot imagine for what purpose, either. A librarian pointed me to an encyclopedia of gems, and amethyst seems to have next to no purpose; it's used for certain specialty types of glass and lenses, of all things.

If I were to speculate, I would guess that these amethyst crystals and the media they so resemble are one and the same, as I wrote of last time.-page.lore.cardamom2.n5ØIf this is true, the secrecy, not to mention the prefect's aversion to questioning, may be because this is an original piece of research the Grand Library is not eager to let into the hands of enemy factions.

However, this theory does not sit quite right with me. The amethyst I handled in the cave and the crystals of media Amanita has shown to me do seem quite similar, but not identical. I would like to see them side-by-side to be sure, but media has a peculiar buzzing or rumbling feel beneath the fingers that amethyst does not.-page.lore.cardamom2.n6€It is quite possible I was unable to sense it on the amethyst in the cave due to the stress of being undergound-- my hands were shaking the one time I managed to touch some, and the feeling is very light --but it does not seem the same to me. The light reflects slightly differently.

I suppose if I ever manage to get my hands on a crystal of amethyst outside of a cave, I will ask Amanita to see if she can cast a spell with it. Every time we meet she seems to have some new fantastic trick.-page.lore.cardamom2.n7ÀJust last week she suspended me in the air supported by nothing at all! It is an immensely strange feeling to have your body tingling and lighter than air with your clothing still the same weight... I am just glad she tugged me over my bed before the effect ran out.

Yours,
-- Cardamom Steles)entry.lore.cardamom2'Cardamom Steles, #2-page.lore.cardamom3.n1Ÿ{i|Full title: Letter from Cardamom Steles to her father, #3, part 1/2}

Dear Papa,
Two very peculiar things have happened since I last wrote.

Firstly, the professor in charge of the entry-level Hexcasting Corps students has disappeared. Nobody knows where he has gone. His office and living quarters were found locked, but still in their usual state of disarray.-page.lore.cardamom3.n2ïEven more peculiarly, any attempts by the students of the Grand to rouse the administrative portions of the gnarled bureaucracy have been very firmly rejected. Even other professors seem reluctant to talk about him.

As you might imagine, Amanita is sorely distressed. Whatever replacement professors the Grand managed to dredge up have none of the old professor's tact or skill with beginners.-page.lore.cardamom3.n3ﬂBut amazingly, that is not the stranger of the two things I have to tell you. The most horrendous thing I hope to ever experience happened on another trip out with the Geology Corps. This time, we were due for an expedition near a village.-page.lore.cardamom3.n4çUsually when we do such a thing, there is a long process of communication with the mayor or elder of the village to ensure we have permission and establish boundaries on where we are allowed to go and what we are allowed to do. But on this expedition, there was very little of that; we were notified where we were going by a prefect of the Hexcasting Corps scarcely two days before we left.-page.lore.cardamom3.n5µWe camped near the village, but in a thick forest, even though the nearby plains would have been much more hospitable. We could barely see the village from where we pitched our tents. As I laid down my bedroll the evening we arrived, the peculiar silence troubled me. Even if we couldn't see the village, we should have been able to hear it. But the whole time we were above-ground, there was next to no sound.-page.lore.cardamom3.n6”The few things I did hear all sounded like work: the peal of hammers on anvils and the scrape of hoe on dirt, for example. I never heard a shred of conversation.

The next morning we readied our lanterns and descended into the earth.-page.lore.cardamom3.n7ı	We weren't told exactly what it was we were spelunking for, but one of the other students had overheard we were looking for more amethyst, which seemed reasonable enough. I had my eyes trained for any specks of purple I might find in the cave walls, but just as the gray stone was making way to black slate, an incredible sight unfolded before me.

It was an entire chamber made of amethyst, nearly ten times as tall as I am. The inside seemed to glow with purple sparks and lanternlight glint, every surface covered with jagged crystal. There was more amethyst here than our entire group had ever excavated since I came to the Grand.-page.lore.cardamom3.n8óGloves were distributed and we were told to get to mining. One of the prefects along with us had a peculiar lavender box I've seen some of the higher-ups in the Grand using for storage, and the other students and I dutifully got to shattering the glassy crystals off the walls of the cave and putting them in the box. Under the outer layers of brittle crystal there seemed to be two types of denser growth. One of them seemed of similar composition to the loose crystal, but one seemed more ... I struggle to find the word.-page.lore.cardamom3.n9õ	I hesitate to say "important," but that's the best I can think of. It had a certain ... gravitas, like the dark, sunken X in its surface held some sacred meaning. Whatever the reason we were under strict instructions not to touch them. Occasionally a misplaced pickaxe would shatter one, and the student responsible would get quite the earful. Although the labor was hard and took most of my attention, I couldn't help but notice how ... lucid I felt. It was a strange mix of feelings: I felt incredibly clear-headed, but I also felt if I stopped to examine the feeling I might never stop./page.lore.cardamom3.n10ÌIt was like each breath in erected a friendly signpost in my head promising the way forward, pointing directly down a steep cliff. I shook my head and immersed myself in the work of mining, which seemed to stave off the signposts.

I did manage, however, to hide a shard of the crystal in my knapsack.

We spent nearly the whole day mining, excavating most of the crystal by the time the prefects' chronometer said the sun would set soon./page.lore.cardamom3.n11ÌAs we left, I couldn't help but notice that on the surfaces of those dark, scored places we left unmined, there seemed to be the faintest buds of new crystal, like they were somehow growing out of them. Everything I had learned about the geology of crystals said they took thousands of years to grow, but here there was new growth in less than a day. I suppose the prefects' warnings against breaking those spots were warranted, at least./page.lore.cardamom3.n12”Our journey back to the surface was uneventful, and we got back to our tents just as the sun was setting-- My apologies, I am nearly out of paper for this letter. There's only so much you can write on one Akashic letter ... This tale is worth purchasing another letter for. I'll send them both at once, so they should arrive together.

Yours,
-- Cardamom Steles)entry.lore.cardamom3'Cardamom Steles, #3-page.lore.cardamom4.n1È{i|Full title: Letter from Cardamom Steles to her father, #3, part 2/2}

Dear Papa,
As I was saying, I was running out of paper to write my story, so the rest of it is in this letter. We made it back to camp just as the sun was setting. And that night was the most horrible event of the whole strange outing.-page.lore.cardamom4.n2øI had gotten up in the middle of the night to relieve myself. The moon was covered with clouds, and I confess I got lost in the winds of the forest and could not find the way back to the camp. Fearing the monsters of the night, I decided I would find my way to the village and see if I could find a bed there. At the least, I would be protected there.-page.lore.cardamom4.n3õThe village was easy enough to find, though there was very little sound. Even this late at night I would expect the inn to be, if not bustling, at least not silent. But peeking through the inn door I saw absolutely nobody.

I knocked on the door of one of the houses to no response. The next two houses, too, seemed completely empty.-page.lore.cardamom4.n4ÌMy pulse started to rise, and I resolved to enter the next house. I figured whoever might be inside would be understanding of their rest being disturbed. At the least, hearing another voice would have been reassuring, even if they didn't let me stay the night under their roof.

The house was very small, barely more than a cartographer's table and a bed. I could see there was someone in the bed, and I tried to reassure myself that everyone in the village was just deeply asleep as I turned to leave.-page.lore.cardamom4.n5ùBut then the clouds shifted, and moonlight glinted across the bed's occupant.

I screamed, and its eyes snapped open. It was ... distinctly, horrendously not human. It was like some awful de-evolution of a man, its forehead too high, its body stocky and dense. I believe it is appropriate to say "it," at least; the thing before me was obviously not as wise as a human, despite how it resembled us.-page.lore.cardamom4.n6ÂIts eyes trained on me-- oh, its eyes were awful, dull and unintelligent like a sheep's! It opened its mouth but a pained mockery of speech poured out, a shuddering, nasal groan.-page.lore.cardamom4.n7ÛI ran. In the light of the newly-revealed moon I caught glimpses of other townspeople through windows, and they were all warped and simplified as the first {i|thing} I had seen. I sprinted into the darkness of the forest, away from those terrible, terrible animal eyes in those distorted faces.

The camp was easier to find now that I could see in the moonlight. No-one seemed to have noticed my prolonged absence, thankfully. I crawled back into my bedroll and did my very best to forget the whole night.-page.lore.cardamom4.n8üAs you can tell from this letter, I did not do a very good job. That warped visage still haunts my dreams. I shudder to think that it once might have been human.

After we got back to the Grand I showed the shard of crystal I had smuggled out to Amanita. She confirmed my suspicions: it is definitely a crystal of media. What an enormous geode full of it is doing underground, though, is beyond her.-page.lore.cardamom4.n9ÎShe also mentioned something interesting: apparently media can be used in a similar way to true amethyst in those niche glasses I mentioned a few letters ago. The physical manner in which they both crystallise happens to be nearly identical, and it has nothing to do with media's magical properties, or so she says.

I chose not to tell her of the village full of monsters./page.lore.cardamom4.n10ÉI know how tight money is for you, and how expensive it is to send a letter all the way back to the Grand, but I beg of you, please send a word of advice back. I am greatly distraught, and reading your words would do me much good.

Yours,
-- Cardamom Steles)entry.lore.cardamom4'Cardamom Steles, #4-page.lore.cardamom5.n1◊{i|Full title: Letter from Cardamom Steles to her father, #4}

Amanita has disappeared.

I don't know where she has gone, Papa. The last I saw her was over dinner, and she had just spoken to someone about the disappearances, and then---page.lore.cardamom5.n2•then-- then she was gone too. And no one speaks of her, and I am so so scared, Papa, do they all know? Everyone must have a friend who's just {i|vanished}, into thin air, into non-being.

Where did they {i|go}?-page.lore.cardamom5.n3«They keep shutting things down, too-- we haven't been on a trip for the Geology Corps in weeks, all the apparati that collect media in the main dome are gone, the Apothecary Corps haven't been open for months... it's like termites are eating the Grand from the inside, leaving a hollow shell.

I think they've started scanning the letters, we write too...-page.lore.cardamom5.n4≠This letter has taken so much courage to write, and I don't have the courage to tell people myself, but if no one here can hold the knowledge I hope and pray you can send the word out... it's a vain hope for this to spread from somewhere as backwater as Brackenfalls, but please, please, do your best. Remember them, Papa... Amanita Libera, Jasmine Ward, Theodore Cha... please, remember them... and please forgive my cowardice, that I foist the responsibility onto you.-page.lore.cardamom5.n5Éi can no longer write, my hands shake so much, please, rescue us.)entry.lore.cardamom5'Cardamom Steles, #51page.lore.experiment1.n1˚{i|I only managed to find these five entries from this log.}

Detonation #26
