Effects on inhabitants still consistently more severe than with single-target testing, especially the physical effects.-entry.lore.experiment1-Wooleye Instance Notes1page.lore.experiment2.n1á{i|These documents were heavily redacted. I have copied the readable text from them here.}

Subject #1 "A.E."
Stopped struggling immediately after procedure. Facial expression and limbs slack, but can stand unassisted. When left unattended, absently pantomimes actions commonly done in previous profession (groundskeeping).1page.lore.experiment2.n2ÔHeartrate high immediately after procedure, but this is inconclusive due to state of fear immediately before. Resulting bud produced 35 uŒ∏/min.
...
Subject #4 "P.I."
Psychological tests run on P.I. Subject has object permanence, spatial awareness, basic numerical reasoning. Difficulty learning new tasks.

...1page.lore.experiment2.n3ÂSubject #7 "T.C."
Similar results several hours after the procedure to other subjects: able to stand, perform simple tasks...

Subject #11 "R.S."
Sedated before procedure...

...1page.lore.experiment2.n4üSubject #23 "A.L."
Ability to speak retained to a greater degree than most subjects; dwindled to broken sentences, then a single word "card" over the course of several hours.

For further testing: how does the procedure affect previous Hexcasters vs. non-Hexcasters?

...-entry.lore.experiment2-Wooleye Interview Logs-page.lore.inventory.n1ÀCell 39, Restoration Log #72, Detainment Center Beta

Prisoner Name: Raphael Barr
Crime: Knowledge of Project Wooleye
Reason for Cell Vacancy: Death
Additional notes: The following letter was scrawled over most of the wall space.-page.lore.inventory.n2˝I see hexagons when I close my eyes.

The patterns, they invade the space between my eyes and my eyelids, my mind, my dreams. I sparkle in and out of lucidity, like a crystal dangling from a string, sometimes catching the light, sometimes consumed by it.-page.lore.inventory.n3—I am more lucid today. Maybe. I cannot tell anymore. I cannot even say I am tired anymore; at some point the constant companion of exhaustion left me, even as something else came to prick at my eyes. I can't sense the fatigue. But it's there.

My bones are fragile. My joints are rough and sharp.-page.lore.inventory.n4ŸSometimes why I am here comes back to me. I remember being too loud about something I knew ... I remember a very bright room where I was told things. I remember my thoughts freezing into glass, shattered, melted and recrystallized over and over and over and over and over with a purpose behind them to make me forget worse than that to keep me alive while killing me, my self, the iota of ME being meaningless because there would be no observer just a body but I tricked them I did it somehow-page.lore.inventory.n5πthey thought they broke me beyond the point of pulling the wool over my eyes but i was awake enough and am awake enough to feel PAIN

I do not sleep but when i wake up I cannot rub the crust off of my eyes because it would cut my skin and I do not want to see the purple glints inside-page.lore.inventory.n6ﬂThey do not kill me, because my husband has my focus, and he would know if I died. But he is no Hexcaster and could not find me with his mediocre skill. i am out of ambit

it h urts to think. quite literally. the thoughts are so wasteful now the leftover striates directly onto the million microcrystals-page.lore.inventory.n7…i remember the doctors in the bright room forcing me to inhale something like sand but sharper and it hurt so much. At first just the physical pain of mucous membranes trying to absorb shatterglass but then they got their fingernails into my stimulus-response and they could do it with a word

i remember camping out and seeing the corps setting up their circle all around a village and the ground under my feet rumbling-page.lore.inventory.n8πdrift out of time. Sometimes I believe I see visions of the future, because they seem to make sense but cannot happen now because I know i will be here until forever because the white room men said so. i see myself toppling over and my skull cracking open into halves and inside will be spears of not-amethyst dripping with blood piercing the wrinkled three pounds of fat and meat dreaming that it is a butterfly-page.lore.inventory.n9Éi hope my students are alright. why do i think that? waste. they told me i'm a waste, they couldn't be content with destroying me they had to make me feel like I deserved it the whole time, too. No sticks or stones to break my bones, just words to hurt me. if they released me no one would believe me because my body is inspectable fully i just look like one more addicted to overcasting

But they locked me up insted and i dont know if it's a mercy/page.lore.inventory.n10Ûwith all the media around I tried many times to cast a hex and get me out or at the least snuff out my suffering but the patterns that march through the fields of my mind snicker and dissolve when I try to reach for them. i think i remember being forced to forget them, I remember grand structures of knowledge interlinked getting chipped away and splintering as it fell apart under the weight of forced ignorance but it hurts so much to try to remember forgetting what you remembered you thought you knew/page.lore.inventory.n11˚maybe I am just in the late late late late stages of overcasting dependency, the patterns papercutting into the space between my eyes and my eyelids I have heard of, the purple edges of my nerves i have heard of. is there any point trying to make myself believe what is true I am not being tortured. I deserve this. if i will never have anyone to discuss it with ever again why try/page.lore.inventory.n12≥they're going to kill everyone n the whole world aren't they the grand needs to eat just as much as i ... when did i lasst eat

everyone else has to eat and they cannot do that if all the farmers in the world are empty and all the knowledge of farming is underground or at least someone else is going to Find out and melt their smug faces to wax/page.lore.inventory.n13’maybe wake up someday and wonder about all the thngs we left them and wonder why there are million miles of tunnels underground with no one smart enough to mine them

i can see them reading this . they ... will be too far gone to care)entry.lore.inventory'Restoration Log #723page.advanced_escaping.n1±
{link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Intro-}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Retrospection}} are very useful, but when building complex {#b38ef3|Hexes} I may need more advanced techniques.

This section describes such techniques: {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/advanced_escaping|{#fc77be|Interjection}} lets me insert iotas directly into an in-progress list, while {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/advanced_escaping|{#fc77be|Meditation}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/advanced_escaping|{#fc77be|Recollection}} let me start and finish the list creation process with arbitrary levels of nesting depth.Opage.advanced_escaping.read_into_parensÖOnly works while {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|introspecting}}. Reads an iota from my offhand, and adds it to the in-progress list. For iotas other than patterns, I may need to {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|take steps}} to avoid a mishap when executing the list.Eaction.hexcasting.read_into_parensInterjectionIpage.advanced_escaping.open_n_parensÖRemoves a number from the stack, then acts as though I'd drawn that many {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Introspections}}. Treated as a normal pattern while {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|introspecting}}, so nesting this may require {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|tricks}} to draw extra {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Retrospections}}.?action.hexcasting.open_n_parensMeditationOpage.advanced_escaping.close_all_parensÕWorks like {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Retrospection}}, but closes {i|all} open {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Introspections}} at once. The number of layers closed, along with the list of saved patterns, are then added to the stack.Eaction.hexcasting.close_all_parensRecollection/entry.advanced_escaping#Advanced Escaping-page.advanced_math.sinÌTakes the sine of an angle in radians, yielding the vertical component of that angle drawn on a unit circle. Related to the values of {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|œÄ}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|œÑ}}.+action.hexcasting.sin#Sine Purification-page.advanced_math.cosıTakes the cosine of an angle in radians, yielding the horizontal component of that angle drawn on a unit circle. Related to the values of {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|œÄ}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|œÑ}}.+action.hexcasting.cos'Cosine Purification-page.advanced_math.tanœTakes the tangent of an angle in radians, yielding the slope of that angle drawn on a circle. Related to the values of {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|œÄ}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|œÑ}}.+action.hexcasting.tan)Tangent Purification3page.advanced_math.arcsinÔTakes the inverse sine of a value with absolute value 1 or less, yielding the angle whose sine is that value. Related to the values of {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|œÄ}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|œÑ}}.1action.hexcasting.arcsin3Inverse Sine Purification;action.book.hexcasting.arcsin%Inverse Sine Prfn.3page.advanced_math.arccos˜Takes the inverse cosine of a value with absolute value 1 or less, yielding the angle whose cosine is that value. Related to the values of {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|œÄ}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|œÑ}}.1action.hexcasting.arccos7Inverse Cosine Purification;action.book.hexcasting.arccos)Inverse Cosine Prfn.3page.advanced_math.arctanøTakes the inverse tangent of a value, yielding the angle whose tangent is that value. Related to the values of {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|œÄ}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|œÑ}}.1action.hexcasting.arctan9Inverse Tangent Purification;action.book.hexcasting.arctan+Inverse Tangent Prfn.5page.advanced_math.arctan2˚Takes the inverse tangent of a Y and X value, yielding the angle between the X-axis and a line from the origin to that point.3action.hexcasting.arctan29Inverse Tangent Distillation=action.book.hexcasting.arctan2+Inverse Tangent Dstl.9page.advanced_math.logarithmßRemoves the number at the top of the stack, then takes the logarithm of the number at the top using the other number as its base. Related to the value of {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/consts|{#8d6acc|{i|e}}}.7action.hexcasting.logarithm1Logarithmic Distillation'entry.advanced_math)Advanced MathematicsEpage.akashic_patterns.akashic.readëRead the iota associated with the given pattern out of the {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/akashiclib|{accent|Akashic Library}} with its {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/akashiclib|{accent|Record}} at the given position. This has no range limit. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.=action.hexcasting.akashic.read+Akasha's DistillationGpage.akashic_patterns.akashic.writeèAssociate the iota with the given pattern in the {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/akashiclib|{accent|Akashic Library}} with its {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/akashiclib|{accent|Record}} at the given position. This {i|does} have a range limit. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.?action.hexcasting.akashic.writeAkasha's Gambit-entry.akashic_patterns!Akashic Patterns=page.basics_pattern.get_casterEAdds me, the caster, to the stack.9action.hexcasting.get_caster#Mind's ReflectionEpage.basics_pattern.entity_pos.eyeÕTransforms an entity on the stack into the position of its eyes. I should probably use this on myself.Aaction.hexcasting.entity_pos.eye+Compass' PurificationGpage.basics_pattern.entity_pos.foot„Transforms an entity on the stack into the position it is standing. I should probably use this on other entities.Caction.hexcasting.entity_pos.foot1Compass' Purification IIGpage.basics_pattern.get_entity_lookØTransforms an entity on the stack into the direction it's looking in, as a unit vector.Caction.hexcasting.get_entity_look-Alidade's Purification=page.basics_pattern.raycast.n1µCombines two vectors (a position and a direction) into the answer to the question: If I stood at the position and looked in the direction, what block would I be looking at? Costs a negligible amount of {#74b3f2|media}.3action.hexcasting.raycast+Archer's Distillation=page.basics_pattern.raycast.n2˚If it doesn't hit anything, the vectors will combine into {#8d6acc|Null}.

A common sequence of patterns, the so-called "raycast mantra," is {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Mind's Reflection}}, {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Compass' Purification}}, {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Mind's Reflection}}, {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Alidade Purification}}, {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Archer's Distillation}}. Together, they return the vector position of the block I am looking at.Gpage.basics_pattern.raycast.axis.n1ﬂLike {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Archer's Distillation}}, but instead returns a vector representing the answer to the question: Which {i|side} of the block am I looking at? Costs a negligible amount of {#74b3f2|media}.=action.hexcasting.raycast.axis1Architect's DistillationGpage.basics_pattern.raycast.axis.n2ˇMore specifically, it returns the {i|normal vector} of the face hit, or a unit vector pointing perpendicular to the face.
‚Ä¢ If I am looking at a floor, it will return (0, 1, 0).
‚Ä¢ If I am looking at the south face of a block, it will return (0, 0, 1).Epage.basics_pattern.raycast.entity·Like {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/basics|{#fc77be|Archer's Distillation}}, but instead returns the {i|entity} I am looking at. Costs a negligible amount of {#74b3f2|media}.Aaction.hexcasting.raycast.entity)Scout's Distillation3page.basics_pattern.printSDisplays the top iota of the stack to me./action.hexcasting.printRevealKpage.basics_pattern.get_entity_heighteTransforms an entity on the stack into its height.Gaction.hexcasting.get_entity_height5Stadiometer's PurificationQaction.book.hexcasting.get_entity_height'Stadiometer's Prfn.Opage.basics_pattern.get_entity_velocityìTransforms an entity on the stack into the direction in which it's moving, with the speed of that movement as that direction's magnitude.Kaction.hexcasting.get_entity_velocity#Pace Purification;page.basics_pattern.get_mediaüPushes the amount of {#74b3f2|media} available to the current cast, in units of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.7action.hexcasting.get_media!Media Reflection)entry.basics_patternBasic Patterns?page.circle_patterns.disclaimer∑These patterns must be cast from a {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/spellcircles|{accent|Spell Circle}}; trying to cast them through a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|Staff}} will fail rather spectacularly.Opage.circle_patterns.circle.impetus_pos€Returns the position of the {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Impetus}} of this spell circle.Iaction.hexcasting.circle.impetus_pos'Waystone ReflectionOpage.circle_patterns.circle.impetus_dirçReturns the direction the {link=@entry:hexcasting:greatwork/impetus|{accent|Impetus}} of this spell circle is facing as a unit vector.Iaction.hexcasting.circle.impetus_dir)Lodestone ReflectionMpage.circle_patterns.circle.bounds.minØReturns the position of the lower-north-west corner of the bounds of this spell circle.Mpage.circle_patterns.circle.bounds.maxØReturns the position of the upper-south-east corner of the bounds of this spell circle.+entry.circle_patterns+Spell Circle Patterns9action.hexcasting.const.trueTrue Reflection;action.hexcasting.const.false!False Reflection9action.hexcasting.const.null%Nullary Reflection=action.hexcasting.const.vec.n0-Vector Reflection ZeroEaction.hexcasting.const.double.tau'Circle's ReflectionCaction.hexcasting.const.double.pi!Arc's ReflectionAaction.hexcasting.const.double.e%Euler's ReflectionEaction.hexcasting.const.double.phi%Harmony Reflectionentry.constsConstants1page.entities.get_entityŸTransform the position on the stack into the entity at that location (or {#8d6acc|Null} if there isn't one).9action.hexcasting.get_entity'Entity PurificationGaction.hexcasting.get_entity.animal7Entity Purification: AnimalQaction.book.hexcasting.get_entity.animal)Entity Prfn.: AnimalIaction.hexcasting.get_entity.monster9Entity Purification: MonsterSaction.book.hexcasting.get_entity.monster+Entity Prfn.: MonsterCaction.hexcasting.get_entity.item3Entity Purification: ItemMaction.book.hexcasting.get_entity.item%Entity Prfn.: ItemGaction.hexcasting.get_entity.player7Entity Purification: PlayerQaction.book.hexcasting.get_entity.player)Entity Prfn.: PlayerGaction.hexcasting.get_entity.living7Entity Purification: LivingQaction.book.hexcasting.get_entity.living)Entity Prfn.: LivingIaction.hexcasting.zone_entity.animal3Zone Distillation: AnimalSaction.book.hexcasting.zone_entity.animal%Zone Dstl.: AnimalQaction.hexcasting.zone_entity.not_animal;Zone Distillation: Non-Animal[action.book.hexcasting.zone_entity.not_animal-Zone Dstl.: Non-AnimalKaction.hexcasting.zone_entity.monster5Zone Distillation: MonsterUaction.book.hexcasting.zone_entity.monster'Zone Dstl.: MonsterSaction.hexcasting.zone_entity.not_monster=Zone Distillation: Non-Monster]action.book.hexcasting.zone_entity.not_monster/Zone Dstl.: Non-MonsterEaction.hexcasting.zone_entity.item/Zone Distillation: ItemOaction.book.hexcasting.zone_entity.item!Zone Dstl.: ItemMaction.hexcasting.zone_entity.not_item7Zone Distillation: Non-ItemWaction.book.hexcasting.zone_entity.not_item)Zone Dstl.: Non-ItemIaction.hexcasting.zone_entity.player3Zone Distillation: PlayerSaction.book.hexcasting.zone_entity.player%Zone Dstl.: PlayerQaction.hexcasting.zone_entity.not_player;Zone Distillation: Non-Player[action.book.hexcasting.zone_entity.not_player-Zone Dstl.: Non-PlayerIaction.hexcasting.zone_entity.living3Zone Distillation: LivingSaction.book.hexcasting.zone_entity.living%Zone Dstl.: LivingQaction.hexcasting.zone_entity.not_living;Zone Distillation: Non-Living[action.book.hexcasting.zone_entity.not_living-Zone Dstl.: Non-Living3page.entities.zone_entityÂTake a position and maximum distance on the stack, and combine them into a list of all entities near the position.;action.hexcasting.zone_entity-Zone Distillation: AnyEaction.book.hexcasting.zone_entityZone Dstl.: Anyentry.entitiesEntitiespage.altiora.n1„Summon a sheaf of {#74b3f2|media} about me in the shape of wings, endowed with enough substance to allow gliding.1action.hexcasting.flightAltiorapage.altiora.n2ÈUsing them is identical to using {accent|Elytra}; the target (which must be a player) is lofted into the air, after which pressing will deploy the wings. The wings are fragile, and break upon touching any surface. Longer flights may benefit from {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/basic|{#fc77be|Impulse}} or (for the foolhardy) {accent|Fireworks}.

Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Crystal}}.1page.brainsweep_spell.n1ÀI cannot make heads or tails of this spell... To be honest, I'm not sure I want to know what it does.9action.hexcasting.brainsweepFlay Mind'page.create_lava.n1·Summon a block of lava (or insert up to a bucket's worth) into a block at the given position. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst}}.;action.hexcasting.create_lavaCreate Lava'page.create_lava.n2√It may be advisable to keep my knowledge of this spell secret. A certain faction of botanists get... touchy about it, or so I've heard.

Well, no one said tracing the deep secrets of the universe was going to be an easy time.1page.greater_sentinel.n1ÎSummon a greater version of my {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/sentinels|{#8d6acc|Sentinel}}. Costs about two {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.1page.greater_sentinel.n2ü	The stronger {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/sentinels|{#8d6acc|sentinel}} acts like the normal one I can summon without the use of a Great Spell, if a little more visually interesting. However, the range in which my spells can work is extended to a small region around my greater {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/sentinels|{#8d6acc|sentinel}}, about 16 blocks. In other words, no matter where in the world I am, I can interact with things around my {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/sentinels|{#8d6acc|sentinel}} (the mysterious forces of chunkloading notwithstanding).)page.make_battery.n1…Infuse a bottle with {#74b3f2|media} to form a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/phials|{accent|Phial.}}?action.hexcasting.craft.batteryCraft Phial)page.make_battery.n2›Similarly to the spells for {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/hexcasting|{#fc77be|Crafting Casting Items}}, I must hold a {accent|Glass Bottle} in my other hand, and provide the spell with a dropped stack of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst}}. See {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/phials|this page} for more information.

Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst}}.-page.teleport.great.n1µFar more powerful than {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/basic|{#fc77be|Blink}}, this spell lets me teleport an entity to anywhere in the entire world! No matter the distance, it always seems to cost about ten {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst}}.Aaction.hexcasting.teleport.great!Greater Teleport-page.teleport.great.n2∑The provided vector is an offset, not an absolute position in the world; for example, if I use (1, 0, 0), the entity will end up precisely one block east of its original position.

Unfortunately, it seems that when teleporting myself, I'm unable to remain perfectly focused. This can cause some of my items to splatter out of my inventory on arrival.

I should also note that the target will be forcibly removed from anything inanimate they are riding ... but I've read scraps that suggest animals can come along for the ride, so to speak.9page.weather_manip.lightning˘I command the heavens! This spell will summon a bolt of lightning to strike the earth where I direct it. Costs about three {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Shards}}.7action.hexcasting.lightning!Summon Lightning=page.weather_manip.summon_rainôI control the clouds! This spell will summon rain across the world I cast it upon. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst}}. Does nothing if it is already raining.;action.hexcasting.summon_rainSummon Rain=page.weather_manip.dispel_rain∑A counterpart to summoning rain. This spell will dispel rain across the world I cast it upon. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Shard}}. Does nothing if the skies are already clear.;action.hexcasting.dispel_rainDispel Rain'entry.weather_manip)Weather Manipulationpage.zeniths.n1ÎThis family of spells all impart a positive potion effect upon an entity, similar to the {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/nadirs|{#fc77be|Nadirs}}. However, these have their {#74b3f2|media} costs increase with the {i|cube} of the potency.Apage.zeniths.potion.regeneration˜Bestows {#8d6acc|regeneration}. Base cost is one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per second.Kaction.hexcasting.potion.regeneration%White Sun's ZenithApage.zeniths.potion.night_vision˝Bestows {#8d6acc|night vision}. Base cost is one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per 5 seconds.Kaction.hexcasting.potion.night_vision#Blue Sun's Zenith=page.zeniths.potion.absorptionÛBestows {#8d6acc|absorption}. Base cost is one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per second.Gaction.hexcasting.potion.absorption%Black Sun's Zenith3page.zeniths.potion.hasteÔBestows {#8d6acc|haste}. Base cost is one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per 3 seconds.=action.hexcasting.potion.haste!Red Sun's Zenith9page.zeniths.potion.strengthıBestows {#8d6acc|strength}. Base cost is one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per 3 seconds.Caction.hexcasting.potion.strength%Green Sun's Zenithentry.zenithsZeniths!page.lists.index±Remove the number at the top of the stack, then replace the list at the top with the nth element of that list (where n is the number you removed). Replaces the list with {#8d6acc|Null} if the number is out of bounds./action.hexcasting.index-Selection Distillation!page.lists.slice›Remove the two numbers at the top of the stack, then take a sublist of the list at the top of the stack between those indices, lower bound inclusive, upper bound exclusive. For example, the 0, 2 sublist of [0, 1, 2, 3, 4] would be [0, 1]./action.hexcasting.slice)Selection Exaltation#page.lists.append±Remove the top of the stack, then add it to the end of the list at the top of the stack.1action.hexcasting.append1Integration Distillation'page.lists.unappend«Remove the iota on the end of the list at the top of the stack, and add it to the top of the stack.5action.hexcasting.unappend1Derivation Decompositionpage.lists.addÂRemove the list at the top of the stack, then add all its elements to the end of the list at the top of the stack.+action.hexcasting.add+Additive Distillation+page.lists.empty_listWPush an empty list to the top of the stack.9action.hexcasting.empty_list#Vacant Reflection)page.lists.singletonóRemove the top of the stack, then push a list containing only that element.7action.hexcasting.singleton+Single's Purificationpage.lists.abs«Remove the list at the top of the stack, then push the number of elements in the list to the stack.+action.hexcasting.abs'Length Purification%page.lists.reverseSReverse the list at the top of the stack.3action.hexcasting.reverse/Retrograde Purification'page.lists.index_ofüRemove the iota at the top of the stack, then replace the list at the top with the first index of that iota within the list (starting from 0). Replaces the list with -1 if the iota doesn't exist in the list.5action.hexcasting.index_of-Locator's Distillation-page.lists.remove_fromüRemove the number at the top of the stack, then remove the nth element of the list at the top of the stack (where n is the number you removed).;action.hexcasting.remove_from-Excisor's Distillation%page.lists.replaceØRemove the top iota of the stack and the number at the top, then set the nth element of the list at the top of the stack to that iota (where n is the number you removed). Does nothing if the number is out of bounds.3action.hexcasting.replace)Surgeon's Exaltation-page.lists.last_n_list±Remove {i|num} elements from the stack, then add them to a list at the top of the stack.;action.hexcasting.last_n_listFlock's Gambit!page.lists.splatõRemove the list at the top of the stack, then push its contents to the stack./action.hexcasting.splat-Flock's Disintegration)page.lists.constructµRemove the top iota, then add it as the first element to the list at the top of the stack.7action.hexcasting.construct-Speaker's Distillation-page.lists.deconstructΩRemove the first iota from the list at the top of the stack, then push that iota to the stack.;action.hexcasting.deconstruct/Speaker's Decompositionentry.lists#List Manipulation-page.logic.bool_coerceÀConvert an argument to a boolean. The number {#8d6acc|0}, {#8d6acc|Null}, False, the vector (0, 0, 0), and the empty list become False; everything else becomes True.;action.hexcasting.bool_coerce)Augur's Purification3page.logic.bool_to_numberØConvert a boolean to a number; True becomes {#8d6acc|1}, and False becomes {#8d6acc|0}.Kaction.book.hexcasting.bool_to_number)Numerologist's Prfn.page.logic.notáIf the argument is True, return False; if it is False, return True.+action.hexcasting.not+Negation Purificationpage.logic.or°Returns True if at least one of the arguments are True; otherwise returns False.)action.hexcasting.or1Disjunction Distillationpage.logic.andÉReturns True if both arguments are true; otherwise returns False.+action.hexcasting.and1Conjunction Distillationpage.logic.xorùReturns True if exactly one of the arguments is true; otherwise returns False.+action.hexcasting.xor-Exclusion Distillationpage.logic.ifıIf the first argument is True, keeps the second and discards the third; otherwise discards the second and keeps the third.)action.hexcasting.if%Augur's Exaltation#page.logic.equals”If the first argument equals the second (within a small tolerance), return True. Otherwise, return False.1action.hexcasting.equals+Equality Distillation+page.logic.not_equalsÂIf the first argument does not equal the second (outside a small tolerance), return True. Otherwise, return False.9action.hexcasting.not_equals/Inequality Distillation%page.logic.greaterØIf the first argument is greater than the second, return True. Otherwise, return False.3action.hexcasting.greater)Maximus Distillationpage.logic.less©If the first argument is less than the second, return True. Otherwise, return False.-action.hexcasting.less)Minimus Distillation+page.logic.greater_eq«If the first argument is greater than or equal to the second, return True. Otherwise, return False.9action.hexcasting.greater_eq/Maximus Distillation II%page.logic.less_eq¡If the first argument is less than or equal to the second, return True. Otherwise, return False.3action.hexcasting.less_eq/Minimus Distillation IIentry.logic#Logical Operators!page.math.numvec◊Many mathematical operations function on both numbers and vectors. Such arguments are written as "num|vec".!page.math.add.n1#Perform addition.!page.math.add.n2çAs such:
‚Ä¢ With two numbers at the top of the stack, combines them into their sum.
‚Ä¢ With a number and a vector, removes the number from the stack and adds it to each element of the vector.
‚Ä¢ With two vectors, combines them by summing corresponding components into a new vector (i.e. (1, 2, 3) + (0, 4, -1) = (1, 6, 2)).!page.math.sub.n1)Perform subtraction.+action.hexcasting.sub1Subtractive Distillation!page.math.sub.n2ÛAs such:
‚Ä¢ With two numbers at the top of the stack, combines them into their difference.
‚Ä¢ With a number and a vector, removes the number from the stack and subtracts it from each element of the vector.
‚Ä¢ With two vectors, combines them by subtracting each component.

In all cases, the top of the stack or its components are subtracted {i|from} the second-from-the-top.!page.math.mul.n1UPerform multiplication or the dot product.+action.hexcasting.mul7Multiplicative Distillation5action.book.hexcasting.mul)Multiplicative Dstl.!page.math.mul.n2˚As such:
‚Ä¢ With two numbers, combines them into their product.
‚Ä¢ With a number and a vector, removes the number from the stack and multiplies each component of the vector by that number.
‚Ä¢ With two vectors, combines them into their {link=https://www.mathsisfun.com/algebra/vectors-dot-product.html|dot product}.!page.math.div.n1MPerform division or the cross product.+action.hexcasting.div+Division Distillation5action.book.hexcasting.divDivision Dstl.!page.math.div.n2›As such:
‚Ä¢ With two numbers, combines them into their quotient.
‚Ä¢ With a number and a vector, removes the number and divides each element of the vector by it.
‚Ä¢ With two vectors, combines them into their {link=https://www.mathsisfun.com/algebra/vectors-cross-product.html|cross product}.

In the first and second cases, the second-from-the-top of the stack is divided by the top of the stack.

WARNING: Never divide by zero!!page.math.abs.n1KCompute the absolute value or length.!page.math.abs.n2èReplaces a number with its absolute value, or a vector with its length.!page.math.pow.n1YPerform exponentiation or vector projection.+action.hexcasting.pow%Power Distillation!page.math.pow.n2˚With two numbers, combines them by raising the first to the power of the second.
‚Ä¢ With a number and a vector, removes the number and raises each component of the vector to the number's power.
‚Ä¢ With two vectors, combines them into the {link=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vector_projection|vector projection} of the second-from-the-top onto the top of the stack.

In the first and second cases, the first argument or its components are the base, and the second argument or its components are the exponent.page.math.floor°"Floors" a number, cutting off the fractional component and leaving an integer value. If passed a vector, instead floors each of its components./action.hexcasting.floor%Floor Purificationpage.math.ceil´"Ceilings" a number, raising it to the next integer value if it has a fractional component. If passed a vector, instead ceils each of its components.-action.hexcasting.ceil)Ceiling Purification/page.math.construct_vecÀCombine three numbers at the top of the stack into a vector's X, Y, and Z components (bottom to top).?action.hexcasting.construct_vec#Vector Exaltation3page.math.deconstruct_vecSplit a vector into its X, Y, and Z components (bottom to top).Caction.hexcasting.deconstruct_vec+Vector Disintegration!page.math.moduloÉTakes the modulus of two numbers. This is the amount {i|remaining} after division - for example, 5 %% 2 is 1, and 5 %% 3 is 2. When applied on vectors, performs the above operation elementwise.1action.hexcasting.modulo)Modulus Distillation-page.math.coerce_axialÎFor a vector, coerce it to its nearest axial direction, a unit vector. For a number, return the sign of the number; 1 if positive, -1 if negative. In both cases, zero is unaffected.=action.hexcasting.coerce_axial%Axial Purification!page.math.randomQCreates a random number between 0 and 1.1action.hexcasting.random%Entropy Reflectionentry.mathMathematics#page.meta.eval.n1ıRemove something evaluatable (usually a pattern or an {link=@entry:hexcasting:casting/whats_a_hex|entire {#b38ef3|Hex}}) from the stack, then cast it as if I had drawn the relevant patterns with my {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|Staff}}.-action.hexcasting.evalHermes' Gambit#page.meta.eval.n2”This can be very powerful in tandem with {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|{accent|Foci}}, and according to one esoteric scroll I found it also makes the bureaucracy of Nature a "Turing-complete" system.

Note that any iotas other than patterns in a {#b38ef3|Hex} cast this way will cause a mishap unless {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|escaped}}. In addition, with the energies of the patterns occurring without me to guide them, {i|any} mishap will cause the remaining actions in the {#b38ef3|Hex} to immediately unravel.)page.meta.eval.cc.n1˜Cast something evaluatable from the stack exactly like {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/meta|{#fc77be|Hermes' Gambit}}, except that a unique "Jump" iota is pushed to the stack beforehand.3action.hexcasting.eval.ccIris' Gambit)page.meta.eval.cc.n2£When the "Jump"-iota is executed, it'll jump directly to the end of the evaluatable, skipping any remaining actions.

While this may seem redundant given {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/meta|{#fc77be|Charon's Gambit}} exists, this allows you to exit {i|nested} {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/meta|{#fc77be|Hermes'}} invocations in a controlled way, where Charon only allows you to exit one.

The "Jump" iota will apparently stay on the stack even after execution is finished... better not think about the implications of that.+page.meta.for_each.n1·Remove something evaluatable and a list from the stack, then cast the evaluatable over each element of the list.5action.hexcasting.for_eachThoth's Gambit+page.meta.for_each.n2ÂMore specifically, for each element in the provided list, it will:
‚Ä¢ Create a new stack, with everything on the current stack plus that element
‚Ä¢ Cast the evaluatable iota
‚Ä¢ Save all the iotas remaining on the stack to a list
Then, after all is said and done, pushes the list of saved iotas onto the main stack.

No wonder all the practitioners of this art go mad.#page.meta.halt.n1ßThis pattern forcibly halts a {#b38ef3|Hex}. This is mostly useless on its own, as I could simply just stop writing patterns, or put down my staff.-action.hexcasting.haltCharon's Gambit#page.meta.halt.n2¡But when combined with {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/meta|{#fc77be|Hermes'}} or {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/meta|{#fc77be|Thoth's Gambits}}, it becomes {i|far} more interesting. Those patterns serve to 'contain' that halting, and rather than ending the entire {#b38ef3|Hex}, those gambits end instead. This can be used to cause {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/meta|{#fc77be|Thoth's Gambit}} not to operate on every iota it's given. An escape from the madness, as it were.+page.meta.thanatos.n1ÎIt seems there is a limit to how many patterns can be evaluated within a single {#b38ef3|Hex}. I can use this pattern to deterime how many patterns remain before I reach this limit.5action.hexcasting.thanatos)Thanatos' Reflectionentry.metaMeta-Evaluationpage.numbers.n1√Irritatingly, there is no easy way to draw numbers. Here is the method Nature deigned to give us.;action.book.hexcasting.number)Numerical Reflectionpage.numbers.n2›First, I draw one of the two shapes shown on the other page. Next, the {i|angles} following will modify a running count starting at 0.
‚Ä¢ Forward: Add 1
‚Ä¢ Left: Add 5
‚Ä¢ Right: Add 10
‚Ä¢ Sharp Left: Multiply by 2
‚Ä¢ Sharp Right: Divide by 2.
The clockwise version of the pattern, on the right of the other page, will negate the value at the very end. (The left-hand counter-clockwise version keeps the number positive).

Once I finish drawing, the number's pushed to the top of the stack.1page.numbers.example.n10/This pattern pushes 10.?page.numbers.example.n10.headerExample 1/page.numbers.example.n7CThis pattern pushes 7: 5 + 1 + 1.=page.numbers.example.n7.headerExample 21page.numbers.example.n32_This pattern pushes -32: negate 1 + 5 + 10 * 2.?page.numbers.example.n32.headerExample 35page.numbers.example.n4.n5OThis pattern pushes 4.5: 5 / 2 + 1 + 1.Cpage.numbers.example.n4.n5.headerExample 4page.numbers.n3¡In certain cases it might be easier to just use an {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/abacus|{accent|Abacus}}. But, it's worth knowing the "proper" way to do things.entry.numbersNumber Literals3page.patterns_as_iotas.n1ôOne of the many peculiarities of this art is that {i|patterns themselves} can act as iotas-- I can even put them onto my stack when casting.

This raises a fairly obvious question: how do I express them? If I simply drew a pattern, it would hardly tell Nature to add it to my stack-- rather, it would simply be matched to an action.3page.patterns_as_iotas.n2˝	Fortunately, Nature has provided me with a set of special patterns that I can use to work with other patterns directly.

In short, {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Contemplation}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Consideration}} let me add one pattern to the stack, {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Introspection}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Retrospection}} let me add a whole list, and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Evanition}} lets me undo mistakes during the creation of such a list.Qpage.patterns_as_iotas.runtime_escape.n1”To use {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Contemplation}}, I draw it, then another arbitrary pattern. That second pattern is added to the stack.Aaction.hexcasting.runtime_escapeContemplationQpage.patterns_as_iotas.runtime_escape.n2…One may find it helpful to think of this as "escaping" the pattern onto the stack, if they happen to be familiar with the science of computers.

The usual use for this is to copy the pattern to a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/scroll|{accent|Scroll}} or {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/slate|{accent|Slate}} using {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Scribe's Gambit}}, and then perhaps decorating with them.Apage.patterns_as_iotas.parens.n1£Drawing {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Introspection}} makes my drawing of patterns act differently, for a time. Until I draw {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Retrospection}}, the patterns I draw are saved. Then, when I draw {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Retrospection}}, they are added to the stack as a list iota.9action.hexcasting.open_parenIntrospectionApage.patterns_as_iotas.parens.n2üIf I draw another {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Introspection}}, it'll still be saved to the list, but I'll then have to draw {i|two} {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Retrospections}} to get back to normal casting.;action.hexcasting.close_parenRetrospection7page.patterns_as_iotas.undo˜Finally, if I make a mistake while drawing patterns inside {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Intro-}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Retrospection}}, I can draw {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Evanition}} to remove the last pattern that I drew from the pattern list that is being constructed.-action.hexcasting.undoEvanition;page.patterns_as_iotas.escapeõSimilar to {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Contemplation}}, but acts even when drawn between {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Intro-}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Retrospection}}. This can "escape" other patterns that work between {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Intro-}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Retrospection}}, putting them into the list without any other effects.1action.hexcasting.escapeConsiderationOpage.patterns_as_iotas.further_notes.n1ôBecause {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Consideration}} acts immediately within {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Intro-}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Retrospection}}, I need to draw two of them if I want to actually put {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Consideration}} into a pattern list. The first {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Consideration}} escapes the special behavior of the second, allowing it to go into the list.

{link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas|{#fc77be|Contemplation}}, on the other hand, only needs to be drawn once no matter the depth.Upage.patterns_as_iotas.further_notes.titleFurther NotesOpage.patterns_as_iotas.further_notes.n2ﬂIf an iota other than a pattern is present in a list to be executed by {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/meta|{#fc77be|Hermes' Gambit}} or any other meta-evaluation pattern, it will normally result in a mishap. However, this can be avoided using the patterns described in this section.

Just as with pattern iotas, the patterns described here can be used to "escape" {i|any other} kind of iota, causing it to be pushed to the stack when something tries to evaluate it instead of causing a mishap.Opage.patterns_as_iotas.further_notes.n3ΩThis technique may be useful if I want a {#b38ef3|Hex} to be able to reference a specific iota, such as a complicated vector or an entity reference, without having to construct or obtain it each time.

To get such an iota into a list of patterns in the first place, I can either use {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/advanced_escaping|{#fc77be|Interjection}} while creating the list, or draw a placeholder pattern and then use {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/lists|{#fc77be|Surgeon's Exaltation}} to replace it once the list has been created./entry.patterns_as_iotas#Escaping Patterns+page.readers_guide.n1√I've divided all the valid patterns I've found into sections based on what they do, more or less. I've written down the stroke order of the patterns as well, if I managed to find it in my studies, with the start of the pattern marked with a red dot.

If an action is cast by multiple patterns, as is the case with some, I'll write them all side-by-side.+page.readers_guide.n2œFor a few patterns, however, I was {i|not} able to find the stroke order, just the shape. I suspect the order to draw them in are out there, locked away in the ancient libraries and dungeons of the world.

In such cases I just draw the pattern without any information on the order to draw it in.+page.readers_guide.n3©I also write the types of iota that the action will consume or modify, a "‚Üí", and the types of iota the action will create.

For example, "{u|vector, number} ‚Üí {u|vector}" means the action will remove a vector and a number from the top of the stack, and then add a vector; or, put another way, will remove a number from the stack, and then modify the vector at the top of the stack. (The number needs to be on the top of the stack, with the vector right below it.)+page.readers_guide.n4ù"‚Üí {u|entity}" means it'll just push an entity. "{u|entity, vector} ‚Üí" means it removes an entity and a vector, and doesn't push anything.

Finally, if I find the little dot marking the stroke order too slow or confusing, I can press {#8d6acc|Control/Command} to display a gradient, where the start of the pattern is darkest and the end is lightest. This works on scrolls and when casting, too!'entry.readers_guide1How to Read This Section#page.readwrite.n1˚This section deals with the storage of {#8d6acc|Iotas} in a more permanent medium. Nearly any iota can be stored to a suitable item, such as a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|{accent|Focus}} or {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/spellbook|{accent|Spellbook}}, and read back later. Certain items, such as an {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/abacus|{accent|Abacus}}, can only be read from.

Iotas are usually read and written from the other hand, but it is also possible to read and write with an item when it is sitting on the ground as an item entity, or when in an item frame.#page.readwrite.n2ÖThere may be other entities I can interact with in this way. For example, a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/scroll|{accent|Scroll}} hung on the wall can have its pattern read off of it.

However, it seems I am unable to save a reference to another player, only me. I suppose an entity reference is similar to the idea of a True Name; perhaps Nature is helping to keep our Names out of the hands of enemies. If I want a friend to have my Name I can make a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|{accent|Focus}} for them.'page.readwrite.readïCopy the iota stored in the item in my other hand and add it to the stack.-action.hexcasting.read'Scribe's Reflection)page.readwrite.writeüRemove the top iota from the stack, and save it into the item in my other hand./action.hexcasting.writeScribe's Gambit5page.readwrite.read.entityüLike {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Scribe's Reflection}}, but the iota is read out of an entity instead of my other hand.;action.hexcasting.read.entity3Chronicler's PurificationEaction.book.hexcasting.read.entity%Chronicler's Prfn.7page.readwrite.write.entity£Like {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Scribe's Gambit}}, but the iota is written to an entity instead of my other hand.

Interestingly enough, it looks like I cannot write my own Name using this spell. I get a sense that I might be endangered if I could.=action.hexcasting.write.entity'Chronicler's Gambit/page.readwrite.readableΩIf the item in my other hand holds an iota I can read, returns True. Otherwise, returns False.5action.hexcasting.readable)Auditor's Reflection=page.readwrite.readable.entity≠Like {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Auditor's Reflection}}, but the readability of an entity is checked instead of my other hand.Caction.hexcasting.readable.entity-Auditor's Purification/page.readwrite.writableøIf I could save an iota into the item in my other hand, returns True. Otherwise, returns False.5action.hexcasting.writable+Assessor's Reflection=page.readwrite.writable.entityØLike {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Assessor's Reflection}}, but the writability of an entity is checked instead of my other hand.Caction.hexcasting.writable.entity/Assessor's Purification)page.readwrite.localÂ	Items are not the only places I can store information, however. I am also able to store that information in the {#74b3f2|media} of the {#b38ef3|Hex} itself, much like the stack, but separate. Texts refer to this as the {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#8d6acc|ravenmind}}. It holds a single iota, much like a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|{accent|Focus}}, and begins with {#8d6acc|Null} like the same. It is preserved between iterations of {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/meta|{#fc77be|Thoth's Gambit}}, but only lasts as long as the {#b38ef3|Hex} it's a part of. Once I stop casting, the value will be lost.5page.readwrite.local.titleThe Ravenmind5page.readwrite.write.local„Removes the top iota from the stack, and saves it to my {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#8d6acc|ravenmind}}, storing it there until I stop casting the {#b38ef3|Hex}.;action.hexcasting.write.localHuginn's Gambit3page.readwrite.read.local˝Copy the iota out of my {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#8d6acc|ravenmind}}, which I likely just wrote with {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/readwrite|{#fc77be|Huginn's Gambit}}.9action.hexcasting.read.local'Muninn's Reflectionentry.readwrite'Reading and Writing#page.sets.numlist˝Set operations are odd, in that some of them can accept two numbers or two lists, but not a combination thereof. Such arguments will be written as "(num, num)|(list, list)".

When numbers are used in those operations, they are being used as so-called binary "bitsets", lists of 1 and 0, true and false, "on" and "off".page.sets.or.n1#Unifies two sets.page.sets.or.n2ÒAs such:
‚Ä¢ With two numbers at the top of the stack, combines them into a bitset containing every "on" bit in either bitset.
‚Ä¢ With two lists, this creates a list of every element from the first list, plus every element from the second list that is not in the first list. This is somewhat similar to {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/lists|{#fc77be|Additive Distillation}}.!page.sets.and.n1GTakes the intersection of two sets.!page.sets.and.n2ÔAs such:
‚Ä¢ With two numbers at the top of the stack, combines them into a bitset containing every "on" bit present in {i|both} bitsets.
‚Ä¢ With two lists, this creates a list of every element from the first list that is also in the second list.!page.sets.xor.n1YTakes the exclusive disjunction of two sets.!page.sets.xor.n2ÉAs such:
‚Ä¢ With two numbers at the top of the stack, combines them into a bitset containing every "on" bit present in {i|exactly one} of the bitsets.
‚Ä¢ With two lists, this creates a list of every element in both lists that is {i|not} in the other list.page.sets.notøTakes the inversion of a bitset, changing all "on" bits to "off" and vice versa. In my experience, this will take the form of that number negated and decreased by one. For example, 0 will become -1, and -100 will become 99.!page.sets.to_setMRemoves duplicate entries from a list.1action.hexcasting.unique/Uniqueness Purificationentry.sets	Sets7page.basic_spell.explode.n1ﬂRemove a number and vector from the stack, then create an explosion at the given location with the given power.3action.hexcasting.explodeExplosion7page.basic_spell.explode.n2õA power of 3 is about as much as a Creeper's blast; 4 is about as much as a TNT blast. Nature refuses to give me a blast of more than 10 power, though.

Strangely, this explosion doesn't seem to harm me. Perhaps it's because {i|I} am the one exploding?

Costs a negligible amount at power 0, plus 3 extra {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per point of explosion power.Apage.basic_spell.explode.fire.n1ÈRemove a number and vector from the stack, then create a fiery explosion at the given location with the given power.=action.hexcasting.explode.fireFireballApage.basic_spell.explode.fire.n2œCosts one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}, plus about 3 extra {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}s per point of explosion power. Otherwise, the same as {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/basic|{#fc77be|Explosion}}, except with fire.7page.basic_spell.add_motionÁRemove an entity and direction from the stack, then give a shove to the given entity in the given direction. The strength of the impulse is determined by the length of the vector.
Costs units of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} equal to the square of the length of the vector, plus one for every Impulse except the first targeting an entity.9action.hexcasting.add_motionImpulse-page.basic_spell.blinkøRemove an entity and length from the stack, then teleport the given entity along its look vector by the given length.
Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Shard}} per two blocks travelled./action.hexcasting.blinkBlink1page.basic_spell.beep.n1≈Remove a vector and two numbers from the stack. Plays an {#8d6acc|instrument} defined by the first number at the given location, with a {#8d6acc|note} defined by the second number. Costs a negligible amount of {#74b3f2|media}.-action.hexcasting.beepMake Note1page.basic_spell.beep.n2—There appear to be 16 different {#8d6acc|instruments} and 25 different {#8d6acc|notes}. Both are indexed by zero.

These seem to be the same instruments I can produce with a {accent|Note Block}, though the reason for each instrument's number being what it is eludes me.

Either way, I can find the numbers I need to use by inspecting a {accent|Note Block} through a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/lens|{accent|Scrying Lens}}.#entry.basic_spellBasic Spells7page.blockworks.place_blockÛRemove a location from the stack, then pick a block item and place it at the given location.
Costs about an eighth of one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.;action.hexcasting.place_blockPlace Block7page.blockworks.break_blockﬂRemove a location from the stack, then break the block at the given location. This spell can break nearly anything a Diamond Pickaxe can break.
Costs about an eighth of one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust, or a negligible amount if breaking a {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/blockworks|{#fc77be|Conjured Block}}}} or {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/blockworks|{#fc77be|Conjured Light}}.;action.hexcasting.break_blockBreak Block9page.blockworks.create_water›Summon a block of water (or insert up to a bucket's worth) into a block at the given position. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.=action.hexcasting.create_waterCreate Water;page.blockworks.destroy_waterœDrains either a liquid container at, or a body of liquid around, the given position. Costs about two {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst}}.?action.hexcasting.destroy_waterDestroy Liquid;page.blockworks.conjure_block’Conjure an ethereal, but solid, block that sparkles with my pigment at the given position. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.?action.hexcasting.conjure_blockConjure Block;page.blockworks.conjure_light¡Conjure a magical light that softly glows with my pigment at the given position. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.?action.hexcasting.conjure_lightConjure Light1page.blockworks.bonemeal˜Encourage a plant or sapling at the target position to grow, as if {accent|Bonemeal} was applied. Costs a bit more than one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.5action.hexcasting.bonemealOvergrow+page.blockworks.edifyÎForcibly infuse {#74b3f2|media} into the sapling at the target position, causing it to grow into an {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/edified|{#8d6acc|Edified Tree}}. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst}}./action.hexcasting.edifyEdify Sapling-page.blockworks.ignite˝Start a fire on top of the given location, as if a {accent|Fire Charge} was applied, or set fire to an entity. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.1action.hexcasting.igniteIgnite5page.blockworks.extinguishÂExtinguish blocks in a large area. Costs about six {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.9action.hexcasting.extinguishExtinguish Area!entry.blockworks%Block Manipulationpage.colorizeıI must be holding a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/pigments|{accent|Pigment}} in my other hand to cast this spell. When I do, it will consume the dye and permanently change my mind's coloration (at least, until I cast the spell again). Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.5action.hexcasting.colorize'Internalize Pigment%page.cycle_variantÅCertain items I create seem oddly receptive to the influence of {#74b3f2|media}. By holding a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Cypher}}, {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Trinket}}, {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Artifact}}, {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|{accent|Focus}}, or {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/spellbook|{accent|Spellbook}} in my other hand, I can use this spell to change the appearance of the item. Costs a negligible amount of {#74b3f2|media}.?action.hexcasting.cycle_variant!Caster's Glamourpage.flights.n1ÛAlthough it seems that true, limitless flight is out of my grasp, I have nonetheless found some methods of holding one in the sky, each with their respective drawbacks.

All forms produce a shimmer of excess {#74b3f2|media}; as the spell gets closer to ending, the sparks are shot through with more red and black.page.flights.n2ÔOther forms of flight do exist, of course. For example, a combination of {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/basic|{#fc77be|Impulse}} and {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/nadirs|{#fc77be|Blue Sun's Nadir}} has been used since antiquity for a flight of sorts.

I've also heard tell of a thin membrane worn on the back that allows the ability to glide. From my research, I believe the Great spell {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/great_spells/altiora|{#fc77be|Altiora}} may be used to mimic it.+page.flights.range.n1=A flight limited in its range.=action.hexcasting.flight.range%Anchorite's Flight+page.flights.range.n2πThe second argument is a horizontal radius, in meters, in which the spell is stable. Moving outside of that radius will end the spell, dropping me out of the sky. As long as I stay inside the safe zone, however, the spell lasts indefinitely. An additional shimmer of {#74b3f2|media} marks the origin point of the safe zone.

Costs about 2 {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per meter of safety, though any radius less than 1 meter will still cost 2 {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.)page.flights.time.n1CA flight limited in its duration.;action.hexcasting.flight.time#Wayfarer's Flight)page.flights.time.n2ªThe second argument is an amount of time in seconds for which the spell is stable. After that time, the spell ends and I am dropped from the sky.

Costs about 2 {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per second of flight; I believe it is best suited for travel./page.flights.can_fly.n1ıReturns whether the given player is under the effects of {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/flight|Anchorite's} or {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/flight|Wayfarer's Flight}.Aaction.hexcasting.flight.can_fly-Aviator's Purification/page.flights.can_fly.n2∑Does not detect whether the player can fly by other means.

It is unclear to me what use this has. But I suppose its utility is in the eye of the beholder.entry.flightsFlight9page.hexcasting_spell.basicsùThese three spells each create an {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{#8d6acc|item that casts a {#b38ef3|Hex}}}.
They all require me to hold the empty item in my off-hand, and require two things: the list of iotas (usually patterns) to be cast, and an entity representing a dropped stack of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst}} to form the item's battery.

See {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|this entry} for more information.Epage.hexcasting_spell.craft.cypher•Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethyst}}.=action.hexcasting.craft.cypherCraft CypherGpage.hexcasting_spell.craft.trinket©Costs about five {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethysts}}.?action.hexcasting.craft.trinketCraft TrinketIpage.hexcasting_spell.craft.artifactßCosts about ten {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Charged Amethysts}}.Aaction.hexcasting.craft.artifactCraft ArtifactCpage.hexcasting_spell.recharge.n1≠Recharge a {#74b3f2|media}-containing item in my other hand. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Shard}} per item.5action.hexcasting.rechargeRecharge ItemCpage.hexcasting_spell.recharge.n2ëThis spell is cast in a similar method to the crafting spells; an entity representing a dropped stack of {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst}} is provided, and recharges the {#74b3f2|media} battery of the item in my other hand.

This spell {i|cannot} recharge the item farther than its original battery size.=page.hexcasting_spell.erase.n1«Clears {#b38ef3|Hex}-containing or iota-containing items in my other hand. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per item./action.hexcasting.eraseErase Item=page.hexcasting_spell.erase.n2üThe spell will also void all the {#74b3f2|media} stored inside the item, releasing it back to Nature and returning the item to a perfectly clean slate. This way, I can re-use {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/hexcasting|{accent|Trinkets}} I have put an erroneous spell into, for example.

This also works to clear a {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/focus|{accent|Focus}} or {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/spellbook|{accent|Spellbook}} page, unsealing them in the process.-entry.hexcasting_spell-Crafting Casting Items'page.itempicking.n1ÁCertain spells, such as {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/blockworks|{#fc77be|Place Block}}, will consume additional items from my inventory. When this happens, the spell will first look for the item to use, and then draw from all such items in my inventory.

This process is called "picking an item."'page.itempicking.n2çMore specifically:
‚Ä¢ First, the spell will search for the first valid item in my hotbar to the {i|right of my {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|staff}}}, wrapping around at the right-hand side, and starting at the first slot if my {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/staff|{accent|staff}} is in my off-hand.
‚Ä¢ Second, the spell will draw that item from as {i|far back in my inventory} as possible, prioritizing the main inventory over the hotbar.'page.itempicking.n3≈This way, I can keep a "chooser" item on my hotbar to tell the spell what to use, and fill the rest of my inventory with that item to keep the spell well-stocked.#entry.itempicking%Working with Itemspage.nadirs.n1£This family of spells all impart a negative potion effect upon an entity. They all take an entity, the recipient, and one or two numbers, the first being the duration and the second, if present, being the potency (starting at 1).

Each one has a "base cost;" the actual cost is equal to that base cost, multiplied by the potency squared.page.nadirs.n2ÒAccording to certain legends, these spells and their sisters, the {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/great_spells/zeniths|{#fc77be|Zeniths}}, were "[...] inspired by a world near to this one, where powerful wizards would gather magic from the land and hold duels to the death. Unfortunately, much was lost in translation..."

Perhaps that is the reason for their peculiar names.7page.nadirs.potion.weakness˘Inflicts {#8d6acc|weakness}. Base cost is one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per 10 seconds.Caction.hexcasting.potion.weakness#White Sun's Nadir;page.nadirs.potion.levitation˚Inflicts {#8d6acc|levitation}. Base cost is one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per 5 seconds.Gaction.hexcasting.potion.levitation!Blue Sun's Nadir3page.nadirs.potion.witherÛInflicts {#8d6acc|withering}. Base cost is one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per second.?action.hexcasting.potion.wither#Black Sun's Nadir3page.nadirs.potion.poisonÛInflicts {#8d6acc|poison}. Base cost is one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per 3 seconds.?action.hexcasting.potion.poisonRed Sun's Nadir7page.nadirs.potion.slowness˜Inflicts {#8d6acc|slowness}. Base cost is one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}} per 5 seconds.Caction.hexcasting.potion.slowness#Green Sun's Nadirentry.nadirsNadirs#page.sentinels.n1°{i|Hence, away! Now all is well,
One aloof stand sentinel.}

A {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/sentinels|{#8d6acc|Sentinel}} is a mysterious force I can summon to assist in the casting of {#b38ef3|Hexes}, like a familiar or guardian spirit. It appears as a spinning geometric shape to my eyes, but is invisible to everyone else.#page.sentinels.n2≥It has several interesting properties:
‚Ä¢ It does not appear to be tangible; no one can touch it.
‚Ä¢ Only my {#b38ef3|Hexes} can interact with it.
‚Ä¢ Once summoned, it stays in place until banished.
‚Ä¢ I am always able to see it if I'm close enough, even through solid objects.=page.sentinels.sentinel.createÔSummons my {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/sentinels|{#8d6acc|sentinel}} at the given position. Costs about one {link=@entry:hexcasting:items/amethyst|{accent|Amethyst Dust}}.Caction.hexcasting.sentinel.createSummon Sentinel?page.sentinels.sentinel.destroyπBanish my {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/sentinels|{#8d6acc|sentinel}}, and remove it from the world. Costs a negligible amount of {#74b3f2|media}.Eaction.hexcasting.sentinel.destroyBanish Sentinel?page.sentinels.sentinel.get_posÅAdd the position of my {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/sentinels|{#8d6acc|sentinel}} to the stack, or {#8d6acc|Null} if it isn't summoned. Costs a negligible amount of {#74b3f2|media}.Eaction.hexcasting.sentinel.get_posLocate Sentinel?page.sentinels.sentinel.wayfindèTransform the position vector on the top of the stack into a unit vector pointing from that position to my {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/spells/sentinels|{#8d6acc|sentinel}}, or {#8d6acc|Null} if it isn't summoned. Costs a negligible amount of {#74b3f2|media}.Eaction.hexcasting.sentinel.wayfind!Wayfind Sentinelentry.sentinelsSentinels;page.stackmanip.pseudo.novice≠Removes the first iota from the stack.

This seems to be a special case of {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/stackmanip|{#fc77be|Bookkeeper's Gambit}}.Gpage.stackmanip.pseudo.novice.titleNovice's Gambit)page.stackmanip.swapKSwaps the top two iotas of the stack.-action.hexcasting.swapJester's Gambit-page.stackmanip.rotateØYanks the iota third from the top of the stack to the top. [0, 1, 2] becomes [1, 2, 0].1action.hexcasting.rotateRotation Gambit=page.stackmanip.rotate_reverseçYanks the top iota to the third position. [0, 1, 2] becomes [2, 0, 1].Aaction.hexcasting.rotate_reverse%Rotation Gambit II3page.stackmanip.duplicateKDuplicates the top iota of the stack.7action.hexcasting.duplicate)Gemini Decomposition)page.stackmanip.overüCopy the second-to-last iota of the stack to the top. [0, 1] becomes [0, 1, 0].-action.hexcasting.over'Prospector's Gambit)page.stackmanip.tuckπCopy the top iota of the stack, then put it under the second iota. [0, 1] becomes [1, 0, 1].-action.hexcasting.tuck'Undertaker's Gambit7page.stackmanip.duplicate_n’Removes the number at the top of the stack, then copies the top iota of the stack that number of times. (A count of 2 results in two of the iota on the stack, not three.);action.hexcasting.duplicate_nGemini Gambit+page.stackmanip.n2dupÉCopy the top two iotas of the stack. [0, 1] becomes [0, 1, 0, 1]./action.hexcasting.n2dupDioscuri Gambit3page.stackmanip.stack_lenÛPushes the size of the stack as a number to the top of the stack. (For example, a stack of [0, 1] will become [0, 1, 2].)7action.hexcasting.stack_len%Flock's Reflection3page.stackmanip.fisherman€Grabs the element in the stack indexed by the number and brings it to the top. If the number is negative, instead moves the top element of the stack down that many elements.7action.hexcasting.fisherman%Fisherman's Gambit=page.stackmanip.fisherman.copyıLike {link=@entry:hexcasting:patterns/stackmanip|{#fc77be|Fisherman's Gambit}}, but instead of moving the iota, copies it.Aaction.hexcasting.fisherman.copy+Fisherman's Gambit II/page.stackmanip.mask.n1˜An infinite family of actions that keep or remove elements at the top of the stack based on the sequence of dips and lines.7action.book.hexcasting.mask'Bookkeeper's Gambit/page.stackmanip.mask.n2ëAssuming that I draw a Bookkeeper's Gambit pattern left-to-right, the number of iotas the action will require is determined by the horizontal distance covered by the pattern. From deepest in the stack to shallowest, a flat line will keep the iota, whereas a triangle dipping down will remove it.

If my stack contains {i|0, 1, 2} from deepest to shallowest, drawing the first pattern opposite will give me {i|1}, the second will give me {i|0}, and the third will give me {i|0, 2} (the 0 at the bottom is left untouched).5page.stackmanip.swizzle.n1ÛRearranges the top elements of the stack based on the given numerical code, which is the index of the permutation wanted.3action.hexcasting.swizzle#Swindler's Gambit5page.stackmanip.swizzle.n2ÖAlthough I can't pretend to know the mathematics behind calculating this permutation code, I have managed to dig up an extensive chart of them, enumerating all permutations of up to six elements.

If I wish to do further study, the key word is "Lehmer Code."!entry.stackmanip%Stack Manipulation-page.types.type_equalsáCompare the given iotas and push whether they are of the same type.;action.hexcasting.type_equals/Similarity Distillation5page.types.type_not_equalsãCompare the given iotas and push whether they are of different types.Caction.hexcasting.type_not_equals5Dissimilarity DistillationMaction.hexcasting.compare_item.lenient+Sorter's DistillationKaction.hexcasting.compare_item.strict+Stacking Distillation3page.types.compare_entityçCompare the given entities and push whether they are of the same type.Aaction.hexcasting.compare_entity1Taxonomer's DistillationOaction.hexcasting.compare_block.lenient/Surveyor's DistillationMaction.hexcasting.compare_block.strict1Inspector's Distillationentry.typesTypeslandingÅI seem to have discovered a new method of magical arts, in which one draws patterns strange and wild onto a hexagonal grid. It fascinates me. I've decided to start a journal of my thoughts and findings.

{link=https://forum.petra-k.at/index.php|Forum Link}